addQuestions('trauma', [
  ['What is major trauma?', 'An injury likely to result in permanent disability or death|Any minor soft-tissue injury|A normal response to exercise|An isolated emotional reaction', 0, 'Major trauma is an injury likely to result in permanent disability or death.'],
  ['What does MOI stand for?', 'Mechanism of injury|Management of illness|Measure of intervention|Movement of information', 0, 'MOI means mechanism of injury.'],
  ['What is the main purpose of the major trauma decision tool?', 'Replace every clinical assessment|Identify patients who may benefit from a specialist trauma centre|Determine a patient\'s blood group|Record rehabilitation exercises', 1, 'The trauma tree helps identify and triage patients who may benefit from specialist trauma-centre care.'],
  ['Which is an example of an immediate HEMS dispatch criterion?', 'A small superficial graze|A routine appointment|A person under a train|A mild headache', 2, 'The deck lists a person under a train as an immediate dispatch criterion.'],
  ['Which factors should be considered when interpreting a road traffic collision?', 'Only the patient\'s age|The weather forecast alone|The colour of the vehicle|Impact angle and restraints used', 3, 'Impact angle, vehicle size, restraints and safety mechanisms all affect the energy transfer.'],
  ['What does kinetic energy depend on?', 'Temperature and blood pressure|Mass and velocity squared|Age and height only|Time and distance only', 1, 'The deck gives kinetic energy as one half mass multiplied by velocity squared.'],
  ['What is triage?', 'A method of taking a full medical history|A type of rehabilitation|A method of measuring temperature|Categorising patients by severity and prioritising care', 3, 'Triage prioritises patients according to the severity of their conditions and available resources.'],
  ['What is the focus of Ten Second Triage?', 'Completing a detailed neurological examination|Planning long-term rehabilitation|Identifying the most severely injured and prioritising life-saving interventions|Taking a full medication history', 2, 'Ten Second Triage is rapid and focused on life-threatening injury and life-saving interventions.'],
  ['Which is a Ten Second Triage life-saving intervention?', 'Control of external haemorrhage|Measuring height|Completing a dietary history|Checking visual acuity', 0, 'The two key interventions are haemorrhage control and airway management.'],
  ['What is the P1 triage category?', 'Immediate|Urgent|Delayed|Discharged', 0, 'P1 is the immediate priority category.'],
  ['Who should urgently reassess a patient marked \'not breathing\' during Ten Second Triage?', 'A member of the public only|A clinical responder|The vehicle driver|The hospital receptionist', 1, 'Not-breathing patients require urgent reassessment by a clinical responder.'],
  ['What is over-triage?', 'Failing to record a patient\'s name|Delaying every patient equally|Assigning a higher priority than the patient requires|Using a trauma tag', 2, 'Over-triage places pressure on limited scene resources.'],
  ['What is under-triage?', 'Sending every patient to a major trauma centre|Using a secondary triage tag|Reporting a hazard|Failing to prioritise a patient who needs urgent care', 3, 'Under-triage can prevent a patient receiving medical attention when it is required.'],
  ['What does the M in METHANE represent?', 'Mechanism of injury|Major incident declared|Medical history|Movement', 1, 'METHANE begins with Major incident declared.'],
  ['What does ATMIST support?', 'Airway suction only|A medication chart|A rehabilitation plan|Trauma handover', 3, 'ATMIST is a structured format used for trauma handover.'],
  ['What does the A in ATMIST represent?', 'Airway|Assessment only|Age|Ambulance', 2, 'ATMIST starts with the patient\'s Age.'],
  ['What does SBAR stand for?', 'Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation|Safety, Breathing, Airway, Response|Scene, Cause, Assessment, Report|Signs, Blood pressure, Airway, Respiration', 0, 'SBAR is Situation, Background, Assessment and Recommendation.'],
  ['Why is structured handover important?', 'Missing information can lead to treatment errors and harm|It replaces reassessment|It avoids documenting treatment|It guarantees a normal outcome', 0, 'The deck links incomplete handover with errors in treatment and possible harm.'],
  ['Which is one role of a major trauma centre?', 'Only routine vaccination|Specialist trauma assessment and access to life-saving treatment|Only transport home|Only psychological first aid', 1, 'MTCs provide specialist, consultant-led assessment, diagnostics, treatment and rehabilitation access.'],
  ['Which patient may warrant a lower threshold for a trauma alert?', 'A patient with no injury|A patient attending a routine check|A patient taking anticoagulants|A patient with normal findings only', 2, 'Anticoagulant use is listed as a special consideration.'],
  ['What does SCENE help assess?', 'Only the patient\'s pulse|Only the destination hospital|Only the weather|Safety, cause, environment, number of patients and extra resources', 3, 'SCENE supports the initial trauma scene assessment.'],
  ['What should happen after a life-saving intervention or Ten Second Triage?', 'Stop documenting|Reassess the patient|Ignore new findings|Automatically discharge the patient', 1, 'The trauma pathway depends on repeated reassessment after interventions.']
]);
addQuestions('trauma', [
  ['What is one purpose of pre-hospital trauma care?', 'To delay transport until every diagnosis is confirmed|To replace major trauma centre treatment|To avoid communicating with receiving teams|To provide initial care and move the patient to the most appropriate place', 3, 'Pre-hospital teams provide initial care and help ensure the patient reaches the most appropriate destination.'],
  ['Which finding is listed as a significant mechanism in an adult?', 'A fall from a low chair with no symptoms|Walking a short distance|A fall over 6 metres or two storeys|A minor paper cut', 2, 'The material identifies an adult fall over 6 metres or two storeys as significant.'],
  ['Which fall is listed as a significant mechanism for a child?', 'A fall over 3 metres or twice the child height|A normal playground walk|A small bruise with no mechanism|A routine school journey', 0, 'For children, the deck lists a fall over 3 metres or twice the child height.'],
  ['What does the T in METHANE represent?', 'Type of incident|Treatment given|Time of arrival|Triage tag', 0, 'The T in METHANE identifies the type of incident.'],
  ['When is secondary triage undertaken?', 'Before scene safety is considered|After life-saving interventions and Ten Second Triage, when the Scene Commander decides to commence it|Only after rehabilitation|Instead of airway management', 1, 'Secondary triage follows life-saving interventions and Ten Second Triage.'],
  ['Who undertakes the Major Incident Triage Tool?', 'Only members of the public|The vehicle mechanic|Clinical responders only|The hospital porter', 2, 'The Major Incident Triage Tool is for clinical responders.'],
  ['Which service may contribute to rehabilitation after severe trauma?', 'Vehicle recovery only|Dispatch administration only|Traffic control only|Occupational therapy', 3, 'The deck names occupational therapists among the professionals who may support rehabilitation.'],
  ['What happens to some energy in a vehicle collision?', 'It is completely destroyed at the moment of impact|It can be dissipated by crumple zones and protective devices|It becomes a diagnosis automatically|It changes into sound only', 1, 'Crumple zones, restraints and airbags can dissipate or manipulate energy before it reaches the patient.']
]);
addQuestions('trauma', [
  ['What is the focus of the Trauma Overview session?', 'Replacing all minor-injury training|Managing routine outpatient reviews|Planning long-term community care only|Supporting trauma management in emergency and urgent care', 3, 'The document is designed to support management of trauma in emergency and urgent care settings.'],
  ['Which action is a key message before starting trauma care?', 'Delay all equipment preparation|Complete every intervention on scene|Prioritise scene safety|Wait for vital signs to become abnormal', 2, 'Scene safety is one of the overview document key messages.'],
  ['What should happen to on-scene time in major trauma?', 'It should be kept to a minimum|It should be extended for detailed paperwork|It should be the same for every patient|It should continue until every injury is treated', 0, 'The overview repeatedly emphasises minimal on-scene times.'],
  ['Which phase includes infection and acute respiratory distress syndrome as late causes of death?', 'Late deaths|Immediate fatal injury|Initial scene assessment|Secondary triage only', 0, 'The trauma-death slide lists infection and acute respiratory distress syndrome as late deaths.'],
  ['What is identified as the major cause of death in trauma?', 'Minor skin injury|Blood loss|Delayed rehabilitation|Loss of appetite', 1, 'The overview identifies blood loss as the major cause of death in trauma.'],
  ['How can young healthy trauma patients compensate for blood loss?', 'By immediately stopping all circulation|By lowering their respiratory rate to zero|Through vasoconstriction and increased heart and respiratory rates|By preventing any injury from occurring', 2, 'The document lists vasoconstriction, increased heart rate and increased respiratory rate as compensatory responses.'],
  ['Why can vital signs be misleading after trauma?', 'Vital signs never change after trauma|All trauma patients are already in cardiac arrest|Blood loss always causes immediate hypotension|Young healthy patients may compensate well until late', 3, 'Compensation can make serious blood loss difficult to recognise early.'],
  ['What is the textbook normal heart-rate range shown in the overview?', '10 to 20 per minute|60 to 80 per minute|100 to 140 per minute|160 to 200 per minute', 1, 'The overview gives 60 to 80 beats per minute as the textbook range.'],
  ['What is the textbook normal breathing-rate range shown in the overview?', '2 to 5 per minute|24 to 40 per minute|50 to 60 per minute|12 to 20 per minute', 3, 'The overview gives 12 to 20 breaths per minute as the textbook range.'],
  ['Which risk group is associated with alcohol and drug involvement in the overview?', 'Only newborn babies|Only patients with minor wounds|Young healthy adults|Only people receiving rehabilitation', 2, 'The overview identifies young healthy adults in relation to alcohol and drug involvement.'],
  ['Why are older patients at increased risk after trauma?', 'They may be less resilient and have multiple medical problems and medications|They always have normal examinations|They cannot sustain any injury|They never take medication', 0, 'The overview highlights reduced resilience and multiple medical problems and medications.'],
  ['Why can children be at risk of serious injury?', 'They may have little concept of danger|They always recognise every hazard|They cannot sustain high forces|They never require assessment', 0, 'The document identifies limited awareness of danger as a risk factor for children.'],
  ['Which injury pattern is highlighted in older patients?', 'Only injury after high-speed collisions|Significant injury after a fall from standing|No injury after a fall downstairs|Only superficial injury after any fall', 1, 'The overview states that significant injuries from standing-height falls are more common in older patients.'],
  ['What is important when an older patient has a possible traumatic injury?', 'Avoiding all assessment|Assuming the fall was harmless|Careful neurological assessment|Removing all movement precautions immediately', 2, 'The document specifically calls for careful neurological assessment.'],
  ['What does the overview say about not applying a spinal collar?', 'It always proves the spine is clear|It removes the need for neurological assessment|It confirms there is no head injury|It is not the same as clearing the spine', 3, 'The slide states that not applying a collar is not clearing the spine.'],
  ['Which factors can contribute to serious injuries being missed?', 'A complete and repeated assessment|Unappreciated forces, patient compensation, time to scene, experience and mechanism|Immediate communication with the paramedic|A clearly documented handover', 1, 'The overview identifies time to scene, experience, mechanism, compensation and unappreciated forces as factors in missed injury.'],
  ['What is the suggested on-scene time for penetrating trauma?', 'Several hours|Until all surgery is completed|The same as a routine wound review|Minutes', 3, 'The overview says penetrating trauma should be managed in minutes because it cannot be fixed on scene or in the ambulance.'],
  ['What is the suggested maximum on-scene time for blunt trauma?', '2 hours|60 minutes in every case|20 minutes|No time limit', 2, 'The overview states no more than 20 minutes for blunt trauma.'],
  ['Why should penetrating trauma have minimal on-scene time?', 'It cannot be fixed on scene or in the ambulance|It is always less serious than blunt trauma|It requires prolonged scene paperwork|It never needs hospital treatment', 0, 'The overview explains that definitive treatment for penetrating trauma cannot be provided on scene or in the ambulance.'],
  ['Which preparation helps avoid delay during patient removal?', 'Plan and clear the exit route|Park the ambulance away from the patient|Leave the trolley packed away|Wait until removal begins to find equipment', 0, 'The preparation list includes planning and clearing the exit route.'],
  ['Which item is included in the overview preparation checklist?', 'Turn the heating off|Have the kit and trolley ready|Block the ambulance exit|Delay briefing family and police', 1, 'The checklist includes kit out, trolley out and the ambulance pointing in the correct direction.'],
  ['What is an important principle of scene management?', 'Give every task to one person|Avoid communicating|Match the skill level to the task|Ignore who is holding the head', 2, 'The overview says to involve everyone and match skill level to task.'],
  ['Why are the first 10 minutes of major trauma important?', 'No assessment should occur during that time|It is the only time reassessment is needed|Transport should always be delayed|Life-threatening injuries must be identified and managed rapidly', 3, 'The overview describes the first 10 minutes as vital for consistent rapid assessment and management.'],
  ['What does the overview say about assessment and management in the first 10 minutes?', 'Complete all assessment before any intervention|They should happen simultaneously|Only manage injuries after hospital arrival|Assess the scene without treating the patient', 1, 'The overview states that assessment and management are simultaneous in the first 10 minutes.'],
  ['What does the D in the first DR CAcBCDE step represent?', 'Disability|Diagnosis|Destination|Danger', 3, 'The first D is Danger.'],
  ['What does the R in DR CAcBCDE represent?', 'Resuscitation only|Rehabilitation|Response|Radiology', 2, 'R represents Response.'],
  ['What does the lowercase c in DR CAcBCDE represent?', 'C-spine consideration|Cardiac history only|Capillary refill only|Clinical discharge', 0, 'The overview lists C-spine consideration after Airway.'],
  ['What does E represent in the primary survey?', 'Expose and evaluate|Evacuate only|Exclude every injury|End the assessment', 0, 'E means expose and evaluate.'],
  ['What information is included in the Trauma Priority Call CATMIST?', 'Only the patient name and destination|Call sign, age, time, mechanism, injuries, vital signs and treatment|Only the mechanism and postcode|Only the treatment completed in hospital', 1, 'The overview expands CATMIST as Call sign, Age, Time, Mechanism, Injuries, vital Signs and Treatment.']
]);
addQuestions('trauma', [
  ['When does the overview say 100 percent oxygen should be given to a seriously injured patient?', 'Only after rehabilitation begins|Before checking whether the airway is open|As soon as the airway is clear|Only after discharge', 2, 'The overview states that seriously injured patients should receive 100 percent oxygen as soon as the airway is clear.']
]);
addQuestions('chest-injuries', [
  ['What is the main early problem associated with major chest injuries?', 'Hyperglycaemia|Skin infection|Hypoxia|Dehydration', 2, 'The deck identifies hypoxia from impaired ventilation or hypovolaemia as the most common problem associated with major chest injuries.'],
  ['Which structure may be injured in chest trauma?', 'The heart|The liver only|The knee joint only|The outer ear only', 0, 'Chest trauma can injure the heart, blood vessels, lungs, airways, soft tissues, bones, spinal cord and nerves.'],
  ['What associated injuries should be considered with rib injuries?', 'Abdominal-organ and neck or spinal injuries|Only a minor skin wound|An isolated ear injury|No further injury once the rib is identified', 0, 'The deck lists possible associated injuries to abdominal organs and reminds clinicians to consider neck or spinal injury.'],
  ['What does MOI mean in the assessment of chest trauma?', 'Measure of oxygen intake|Mechanism of injury|Movement of injury|Management of infection', 1, 'MOI means mechanism of injury and helps indicate the likelihood of significant chest injury.'],
  ['Which factors about the mechanism should be considered?', 'The patient clothing and time of day only|The patient height only|The force, energy, duration and direction|The patient preferred destination only', 2, 'The deck specifically highlights the force and energy delivered, and the duration and direction.'],
  ['Which is a possible sign of chest injury?', 'Symmetrical chest movement with no symptoms|Normal oxygenation in every case|Isolated lower-limb tenderness|Abnormal chest-wall movement', 3, 'Possible findings include abnormal chest-wall movement, hypoxia, dyspnoea, wounds, pain and shock.'],
  ['Why can a rib fracture cause inadequate ventilation?', 'It always blocks the upper airway|Pain can lead to shallow breathing|It stops the heart immediately|It increases lung size', 1, 'Pain from rib fractures can cause the patient to breathe shallowly, leading to inadequate ventilation.'],
  ['What does a fractured sternum suggest?', 'That the injury is definitely minor|That no clinical review is needed|That only the arm is injured|A significant probability of other major injuries', 3, 'The force required to fracture the sternum may have caused other major injuries.'],
  ['What is flail chest?', 'Air in the stomach|Blood in the airway only|A segment of chest wall created by two or more rib and/or sternal fractures in two or more places|A superficial chest bruise', 2, 'Flail chest involves two or more rib and/or sternal fractures in two or more places, creating an unsupported segment.'],
  ['What movement may be seen with a flail segment?', 'Inwards on inspiration and outwards on expiration|Inwards on expiration only|No movement during breathing|Upwards only during coughing', 0, 'The deck describes paradoxical movement: inwards on inspiration and outwards on expiration.'],
  ['What is a pneumothorax?', 'Air in the pleural space|Blood in the stomach|Fluid in the ear|A fracture of the sternum', 0, 'A pneumothorax is air between the parietal and visceral pleural layers.'],
  ['What can happen as a pneumothorax enlarges?', 'The lungs become permanently stronger|The lung can collapse|The heart rate must always slow|The chest wall becomes uninjured', 1, 'Increasing pleural air can place pressure on the lung and cause it to collapse.'],
  ['What makes a tension pneumothorax life threatening?', 'It only causes a superficial bruise|It always resolves without treatment|Rising pressure can impair ventilation and venous return|It affects hearing only', 2, 'Pressure can collapse the lung, shift structures and obstruct blood flow back to the heart, causing obstructive shock.'],
  ['Which combination is consistent with possible tension pneumothorax?', 'Normal breathing and improved colour|Bradycardia with a normal chest examination|Isolated ankle pain|Dyspnoea, tachycardia and hypotension', 3, 'The deck describes increasing dyspnoea, tachycardia and hypotension, with reduced breath sounds or chest movement among other signs.'],
  ['Which additional finding may occur with tension pneumothorax?', 'Equal strong breath sounds in every case|Reduced or absent breath sounds|Improved chest movement|A normal chest examination only', 1, 'Reduced or absent breath sounds and reduced chest-wall movement are possible findings.'],
  ['What is an open pneumothorax?', 'Blood is trapped in the pericardium|A rib is bruised without a wound|The airway is blocked by food|A chest-wall wound allows air to enter the pleural space', 3, 'An open pneumothorax follows penetrating trauma when a wound allows air into the pleural space.'],
  ['What is a haemothorax?', 'Air in the stomach|Fluid in the ear|Blood in the thoracic or pleural cavity|A fracture of the airway', 2, 'A haemothorax is blood within the thoracic or pleural cavity.'],
  ['Why can a haemothorax cause both hypoxia and hypovolaemia?', 'Blood occupies space needed by the lungs and represents blood loss|It increases oxygen delivery and blood volume|It only affects the skin|It always causes hyperventilation without blood loss', 0, 'Blood in the chest can compress the lungs while the bleeding causes loss of circulating volume.'],
  ['What happens in cardiac tamponade?', 'Fluid in the pericardial sac compresses the heart|Air enters the pleural space only|A rib moves normally|The lungs over-expand', 0, 'Excessive fluid, often blood after penetrating injury, compresses the heart and limits its ability to pump.'],
  ['Which condition is more commonly associated with penetrating chest injury?', 'Simple wrist sprain|Cardiac tamponade|Tension headache|Minor skin abrasion', 1, 'The deck states that cardiac tamponade is more common in penetrating injury.'],
  ['What is an important early action during the breathing assessment?', 'Cover the chest so it cannot be inspected|Complete a full past medical history first|Expose the chest|Delay assessment until hospital', 2, 'The primary breathing assessment instructs the clinician to expose the chest.'],
  ['Which areas should not be forgotten during chest inspection?', 'Only the patient shoes|Only the forehead|Only the hands|The back and axilla', 3, 'The deck specifically reminds clinicians to inspect the back of the chest and the axilla.'],
  ['What should be checked by listening during the chest assessment?', 'Visual acuity|Air entry in both lungs|Blood glucose only|Knee stability', 1, 'The deck advises listening once to both lungs to confirm air entry.'],
  ['What should be assessed by palpating the chest?', 'Only the patient temperature|Only the pulse in the foot|Only the pupils|Injuries, deformity, instability, tenderness and movement', 3, 'Palpation includes injury, deformity, instability, tenderness, depth and symmetry of movement.'],
  ['What does the deck advise if a major chest problem cannot be corrected?', 'Keep the patient on scene until every test is complete|Discharge the patient|Undertake a time-critical transfer to a major trauma unit|Delay escalation until symptoms disappear', 2, 'Some major chest injuries fail at breathing and require time-critical transfer if the problem cannot be corrected.'],
  ['When should a secondary survey be completed for a time-critical patient?', 'En route to hospital|Only after discharge|Before any primary survey|Only after rehabilitation', 0, 'The deck states that the secondary survey is done en route when the patient is time critical.'],
  ['What initial oxygen delivery is listed in the deck for major trauma?', '15 litres per minute via a reservoir mask|1 litre per minute via a nasal cannula in every case|No oxygen until arrival|50 litres per minute through an unconnected mask', 0, 'The deck lists 15 litres per minute using a reservoir mask as the initial major-trauma oxygen dose.'],
  ['What saturation range is given as the target once vital signs are normal?', '50-60 percent|94-98 percent|70-75 percent|100-110 percent', 1, 'The slide says to reduce oxygen once vital signs are normal and aim for a target saturation of 94-98 percent.'],
  ['What does the deck advise about nitrous oxide and Entonox when pneumothorax is suspected?', 'Give unlimited nitrous oxide|Use Entonox without checking the chest|Do not give nitrous oxide and use caution with Entonox|Avoid all reassessment', 2, 'The deck lists nitrous oxide as contraindicated in clinically suspected pneumothorax and cautions that Entonox may enlarge it.'],
  ['Which is a key principle after applying a chest seal?', 'Stop monitoring after dressing the wound|Delay escalation until the dressing is removed|Apply another dressing without checking the patient|Keep reassessing breathing, oxygenation and circulation', 3, 'Chest-wound management should follow local policy and be followed by reassessment of breathing, oxygenation and circulation.']
]);
addQuestions('drowning', [
  ['Which statement best defines drowning?', 'A respiratory impairment caused by submersion or immersion in liquid|A temporary fear of water without breathing impairment|A cold-related skin injury|A traumatic injury that occurs only below the surface', 0, 'The deck defines drowning as respiratory impairment caused by submersion or immersion in liquid.'],
  ['According to the deck, what does submersion describe?', 'The airway being occluded by liquid|The whole body being completely below the surface|Exposure to cold air after leaving the water|Being covered by a dry protective layer', 0, 'The deck describes submersion as the airway being occluded by liquid; the whole body does not necessarily need to be below the surface.'],
  ['According to the deck, what does immersion describe?', 'An airway blocked by food|Being covered or surrounded by a liquid medium|A spinal injury caused by a fall|A patient who is completely outside the water', 1, 'The deck describes immersion as being covered in a liquid medium, without requiring the entire body to be underwater.'],
  ['What key physiological consequence can follow drowning?', 'Hyperglycaemia caused by cold water|Isolated limb weakness|Hypoxia caused by impaired breathing|A minor skin infection', 2, 'The deck describes drowning as causing asphyxia followed by hypoxia, making airway and breathing priorities.'],
  ['What may happen after a conscious person initially holds their breath in water?', 'The lungs automatically fill with oxygen|The airway becomes permanently protected|Hypothermia is immediately prevented|The urge to breathe takes over and water may enter the lungs', 3, 'The deck describes breath holding followed by an overwhelming urge to breathe and possible entry of water into the lungs.'],
  ['What is laryngospasm?', 'Fluid collecting around the heart|Involuntary closure of the larynx|A fracture of the larynx|A form of hypothermia', 1, 'The deck defines laryngospasm as involuntary closure of the larynx.'],
  ['What can aspirated water wash away?', 'The pericardium|The sternum|The spinal cord|Surfactant', 3, 'Aspirated water can wash away surfactant, which helps keep the alveoli open.'],
  ['What can loss of surfactant cause?', 'Reduced need for ventilatory support|Immediate normalisation of oxygenation|Increased work of breathing|Reduced pressure requirements in every patient', 2, 'Loss of surfactant increases the work of breathing and higher ventilation pressures may be needed.'],
  ['Which event may have precipitated an immersion incident?', 'A convulsion|A routine meal|Improved coordination|A healed skin wound', 0, 'The deck gives a convulsion and traumatic injuries as examples of events that may precipitate an immersion incident.'],
  ['Which factor is identified as increasing risk during a drowning incident?', 'Alcohol or drug intoxication|Early clinical support|A completed risk assessment|A clear route to safety', 0, 'Alcohol and drugs are listed as exacerbating factors.'],
  ['Which factor is particularly important when considering survival after submersion?', 'The colour of the patient clothing|The duration of hypoxia|The distance to the nearest shop|The time of day only', 1, 'The deck identifies duration of hypoxia as a key element in survival.'],
  ['What can cold water and associated hypothermia do in a drowning incident?', 'They make resuscitation unnecessary|They guarantee survival after prolonged submersion|They may extend the period in which survival is possible, but do not remove the need for urgent treatment|They only affect the patient after hospital discharge', 2, 'The deck highlights water temperature and associated hypothermia as factors that may influence survival, while urgent treatment remains essential.'],
  ['According to the deck, does fresh or salt water change the initial management?', 'Only fresh water requires oxygen|Yes, each requires a completely different first response|Only salt water requires airway assessment|No, the initial management is the same', 3, 'The deck states that the historic fresh-water and salt-water distinction does not change initial management.'],
  ['What is the first priority before attempting a water rescue?', 'Removing the patient\'s wet clothing|Safety, including a dynamic risk assessment|Completing a full medical history|Waiting to see whether the patient deteriorates', 1, 'The deck repeatedly emphasises safety first before and during rescue.'],
  ['Which set contains relevant dynamic-risk-assessment factors?', 'Patient name, hospital number and past medical history only|Weather forecast, road traffic and clothing colour|Blood glucose, pain score and medication list only|Number of patients, age, size, time in water, possible air pockets and water temperature', 3, 'The deck lists patient number, age, size, duration, air pockets and approximate water temperature among the risk factors.'],
  ['Why should rescuers keep a safe distance where possible?', 'To prevent the patient being assessed|To avoid communicating with emergency services|To reduce the risk of creating additional casualties|To delay all clinical care', 2, 'Keeping a safe distance is part of the dynamic risk assessment and protects rescuers and other patients.'],
  ['Which communication structure is recommended at the scene?', 'SBAR|A medication chart|A rehabilitation plan|A dietary assessment', 0, 'The drowning deck includes SBAR as part of scene assessment and communication.'],
  ['How should a casualty with a reduced level of consciousness be removed from the water where possible?', 'In a horizontal position|Standing unsupported|Sitting upright on the edge of the water|Face down without monitoring', 0, 'The deck advises removing casualties with reduced consciousness in a horizontal position.'],
  ['What is the stated rationale for horizontal removal of a casualty with reduced consciousness?', 'It guarantees that no water was aspirated|It helps maintain blood pressure and concentrate warm blood in the core|It removes the need for airway assessment|It prevents every form of hypothermia', 1, 'The deck links horizontal removal with hydrostatic pressure, maintaining blood pressure and concentrating warm blood in the core.'],
  ['Which structured approach does the deck use after rescue?', 'A pain score alone|A dietary assessment|DR ABCDE|A musculoskeletal examination alone', 2, 'The deck presents a DR ABCDE approach for assessment after rescue.'],
  ['What injury should be considered after a rescue involving a fall, jump or collision?', 'An isolated skin injury only|No injury because the patient was rescued|A lower-limb injury only|Cervical-spine injury', 3, 'The deck advises considering cervical-spine injury when the rescue mechanism suggests it.'],
  ['What should be addressed early in a post-drowning patient?', 'Long-term rehabilitation planning|Hypoxaemia|Thirst only|A minor bruise only', 1, 'The deck specifically instructs clinicians to address hypoxaemia and monitor airway and breathing closely.'],
  ['Which intervention may be considered when a post-drowning patient needs ventilatory support?', 'A plaster cast|A hearing test|A cold-water immersion|Bag-valve-mask ventilation', 3, 'The deck states to consider BVM ventilation when indicated.'],
  ['Why might pulse oximetry fail to provide a reliable reading after drowning?', 'It measures blood pressure instead of oxygenation|It always reads too high after water exposure|Cold exposure or poor peripheral perfusion may affect the reading|It is unreliable only in warm rooms', 2, 'The deck warns that SpO2 might not read; cold exposure and poor perfusion are relevant clinical reasons.'],
  ['What should be prepared for during the airway assessment?', 'Suction|A routine eye examination|A meal|A lower-limb plaster', 0, 'The deck advises preparing to suction after drowning.'],
  ['What is appropriate for a spontaneously breathing patient after drowning?', 'Close monitoring, treatment of hypoxaemia and hypothermia, and transfer for care|Immediate discharge if the patient can speak|No respiratory-rate assessment if the patient is conscious|Observation only if the water was fresh', 0, 'Spontaneously breathing patients still require close monitoring, treatment of hypoxaemia and hypothermia, and transfer for care.'],
  ['What should be done with wet clothing after rescue?', 'Leave it in place to cool the patient|Remove it to help address hypothermia|Use it to cover the airway|Remove it only after discharge', 1, 'The deck advises removing wet clothing as part of addressing hypothermia.'],
  ['Which temperature measurement may be unreliable after cold-water exposure?', 'The patient age|The number of rescuers|A tympanic reading|The incident time', 2, 'The deck states that tympanic temperature readings may be unreliable.'],
  ['What does the deck specifically advise when a drowning child is not breathing normally?', 'Wait for spontaneous recovery|Only measure temperature|Remove the child from clinical care|Commence resuscitation unless a senior clinician states otherwise', 3, 'The lesson specifically states to commence resuscitation on children unless a senior clinician states otherwise.'],
  ['Why should a drowning patient be transferred for medical assessment even if initially appearing well?', 'Transfer is needed only for paperwork|Complications may develop hours after the incident|A normal initial appearance rules out delayed problems|Transfer immediately prevents all hypothermia', 1, 'The deck warns that complications may develop hours after the incident and advises transfer to care.']
]);
addQuestions('suspension-blast-ballistics', [
  ['Which casualty may be experiencing suspension trauma?', 'A casualty with a minor harness mark and no symptoms|A casualty with an isolated lower-limb injury|A casualty with pre-syncopal symptoms or unconsciousness while suspended in a harness|A casualty with an uncomplicated superficial burn', 2, 'The deck describes suspension trauma in a casualty who develops pre-syncopal symptoms or becomes unconscious while suspended in a harness.'],
  ['Why can immobility in a harness become dangerous?', 'The lower-limb muscles stop assisting venous return|The patient develops an airway obstruction in every case|The patient always becomes hypothermic immediately|The harness prevents all blood from reaching the feet', 0, 'Lower-extremity venous circulation depends on muscle contraction, so immobility can lead to venous pooling and a worsening suspension cascade.'],
  ['According to the deck, when may venous pooling begin during the suspension cascade?', 'After several hours only|Only after the casualty is rescued|Immediately after the harness is fitted in every case|At approximately 3-6 minutes if the legs are dangling and not moving', 3, 'The deck gives an approximate 3-6 minute timeframe for venous pooling to begin when the legs are dangling and not moving.'],
  ['What can worsen obstruction of venous return during suspension?', 'Regular movement of the legs|Harness pressure on the femoral veins and abdominal pressure on the inferior vena cava|A warm drink given before rescue|A correctly fitted harness without abdominal pressure', 1, 'The deck describes increasing harness and abdominal pressure compressing venous structures, including the femoral veins and inferior vena cava.'],
  ['What is the priority for a suspended casualty?', 'Continue observation without planning rescue|Rescue the casualty as soon as it is safely possible|Release the casualty without assessing rescue hazards|Wait for loss of consciousness before calling for help', 1, 'The supplied guidance states that suspended casualties should be rescued as soon as safely possible.'],
  ['What may prolong a conscious casualty\'s tolerance of suspension if immediate release is not possible?', 'Removing monitoring to reduce distraction|Keeping the legs completely still|Giving food before attempting rescue|Elevation of the legs where safely possible', 3, 'The deck states that elevation of the legs by the casualty or rescuer may prolong tolerance where safely possible.'],
  ['Which management approach should be used for suspension trauma?', 'Standard ABC management and early escalation|A full rehabilitation assessment before rescue|Observation without monitoring|A plan based only on the suspension height', 0, 'The deck describes standard UK first-aid ABC management and rescue as soon as safely possible.'],
  ['What is essential before approaching a suspended casualty?', 'A complete medication history|A decision to remove all equipment|A dynamic risk assessment|A decision to delay all support', 2, 'The height, access, equipment, structural stability and rescue capability must be considered before approach.'],
  ['What may cause death after a short drop in hanging trauma?', 'Fracture-dislocation of the upper cervical vertebrae|A blast wave injury|Penetrating injury from debris|Asphyxia caused by compression of the airway and neck blood vessels', 3, 'The deck describes a short drop as causing death through asphyxia from compression of the airway and neck blood vessels.'],
  ['What injury may result from a long drop in hanging trauma?', 'Only a superficial skin wound|Fracture-dislocation of the upper cervical vertebrae|A ruptured tympanic membrane from a shock wave|A temporary cavity from a projectile', 1, 'The deck describes a long drop as causing fracture-dislocation of the upper cervical vertebrae.'],
  ['How should the height of a hanging-related drop be handled?', 'Assume a fixed height definition applies|Ignore the height if there is no external wound|Record the approximate height and include it in handover|Only record it after hospital discharge', 2, 'The deck states there is no definition of the height required for either drop and that the approximate height should be recorded and handed over.'],
  ['What should happen early when managing a hanging incident?', 'Call for help and undertake a dynamic risk assessment|Wait until the patient becomes unresponsive|Move every item at the scene immediately|Delay identifying signs of life', 0, 'The management slide prioritises dynamic risk assessment, identifying signs of life and early help.'],
  ['What may be required to release a hanging patient safely?', 'Waiting until the patient has been discharged|Removing or cutting the ligature when required for assessment and management|Leaving the ligature in place even when the airway cannot be managed|Removing it only when there are no signs of life', 1, 'The deck includes removing or cutting ligatures as part of safely releasing and managing the patient.'],
  ['What takes priority if airway management is required in a hanging patient?', 'Scene preservation always takes priority over the airway|A full secondary survey|Waiting for imaging before intervening|Airway management takes priority over cervical-spine management', 3, 'The deck states that the airway should take priority over C-spine management if required.'],
  ['Why may airway management be difficult after hanging?', 'The patient will always have a normal airway|A hanging incident only affects the limbs|Airway injury and swelling may be present|The airway is protected by the ligature', 2, 'The deck warns that airway injury and swelling may make airway management difficult.'],
  ['What should staff do where CPR is not commenced and the scene is safe?', 'Avoid disturbing the scene where possible to support police investigation|Remove all potential evidence immediately|Leave the patient without assessment|Delay calling for clinical support', 0, 'The deck advises preserving the scene where possible if CPR is not commenced, while patient care remains the priority.'],
  ['What may be required after return of spontaneous circulation following hanging?', 'Immediate discharge|No further airway monitoring|Only a routine limb examination|Specialist on-scene care because the patient may be hypoxic and agitated', 3, 'The deck warns that post-ROSC patients may be hypoxic and agitated and may need specialist care.'],
  ['Which injuries should be considered after hanging?', 'An isolated ankle injury only|Spinal or spinal-cord injury|No spinal injury if circulation returns|A lower-leg fracture only', 1, 'The deck specifically states that spine or spinal-cord injury should be considered.'],
  ['Which is a possible setting for a blast injury?', 'A gas leak or industrial site|Only a sports field|Only a routine clinic|Only a low-energy household activity', 0, 'The deck lists warfare, terrorism, gas leaks, factories, commercial sites, fuel refineries, transport and residential settings.'],
  ['What causes a primary blast injury?', 'Flying fragments only|The patient being thrown against an object only|The blast shock wave|A later infection only', 2, 'Primary blast injury is caused by the blast shock wave.'],
  ['Which injury is associated with primary blast injury?', 'A superficial wound from a knife|A fracture caused only by a fall from standing|A chemical burn from a cleaning product|Blast lung or ruptured tympanic membrane', 3, 'The deck lists blast lung, ruptured tympanic membrane and bowel perforation as possible primary blast injuries.'],
  ['What causes a secondary blast injury?', 'Flying fragments and debris|The blast wave propelling the patient|Radiation only|The patient remaining still', 0, 'Secondary blast injury is caused by flying fragments or debris and may cause penetrating trauma and lacerations.'],
  ['What causes a tertiary blast injury?', 'A projectile creating a temporary cavity|The blast wave propelling the patient against an object|A chemical additive only|A ruptured tympanic membrane caused by pressure alone', 1, 'Tertiary injury occurs when the blast wave propels the patient against objects, causing blunt trauma and fractures.'],
  ['Which injury may be classified as quaternary blast injury?', 'Only a fracture caused by being thrown|Only a laceration from debris|Burns, asphyxiation or toxic-substance exposure|Only a spinal injury from a long drop', 2, 'Quaternary injuries include other explosion-related injuries and illnesses such as burns, asphyxiation and toxic-substance exposure.'],
  ['What does the deck associate with quinary blast injury?', 'Projectile entrance wounds only|Injury from a short drop|Venous pooling in a harness|Effects of additives such as bacteria or radiation, including infection or radiation injury', 3, 'The deck associates quinary injury with additives such as bacteria and radiation, including chemical burns, radiation and infection.'],
  ['How are stab wounds described in the deck?', 'High-velocity trauma with a temporary cavity in every case|Low-velocity, low-energy penetrating trauma|A type of blast shock wave injury|A non-penetrating injury', 1, 'The deck describes stab wounds as low-energy penetrating trauma.'],
  ['Why can a stab wound be serious despite a small external injury?', 'It may cause significant internal damage|A small wound always indicates a superficial injury|It cannot damage internal structures|It only affects the skin', 0, 'The deck warns that stab wounds may leave a small external injury despite significant internal damage.'],
  ['What can a high-velocity projectile create inside the body?', 'Only a superficial skin mark|Only a cervical fracture|A permanent cavity and a temporary cavity|Only a ruptured tympanic membrane', 2, 'The deck describes a permanent cavity along the projectile path and temporary cavitation caused by transferred energy.'],
  ['What can a ricochet do?', 'Guarantee a straight pathway|Make the projectile pathway erratic and increase tissue damage|Prevent energy transfer to the body|Make the exit wound predictable', 1, 'The deck states that ricochet can make the bullet pathway erratic and increase tissue damage.'],
  ['What should be considered when assessing a ballistic injury scene?', 'Only the size of the entrance wound|Only the patient destination|Only whether an exit wound is visible|Scene safety, weapon type, kinetic energy, the crime scene and structures along the projectile path', 3, 'The deck lists scene safety, weapon type, possible anatomical structures along the path, crime scene, patient care and kinetic energy.']
]);
addQuestions('abdominal-conditions', [
  ['Why does abdominal pain require careful assessment?', 'It always identifies one specific diagnosis|It only affects the digestive system|It can range from minor illness to serious or life-threatening disease|It never requires hospital assessment', 2, 'The deck stresses that abdominal pain is common, but the cause may be difficult to identify and can range from minor to life-threatening.'],
  ['Why may an older patient with abdominal pain be at particular risk?', 'Older patients may present atypically and have higher mortality|Older patients cannot develop serious abdominal disease|Pain is always absent in older patients|Age rules out the need for observations', 0, 'The deck highlights atypical presentations and substantially higher mortality in older patients.'],
  ['Why can abdominal pain be referred to another site?', 'The pain must always arise in the skin over the affected organ|Referred pain occurs only after abdominal surgery|Pain can never be referred from an internal organ|Organs may have overlapping innervation', 3, 'The deck explains that localised or referred pain can result from overlapping innervation of organs.'],
  ['What is one purpose of dividing the abdomen into four quadrants?', 'To identify the exact diagnosis without further assessment|To support diagnosis, highlight red flags and aid documentation|To replace the primary survey|To determine the patient blood group', 1, 'Quadrants support diagnosis, highlight red flags and provide a consistent way to document findings.'],
  ['Which is one of the nine abdominal regions?', 'Right hypochondrium|Epigastric|Left lumbar|Hypogastric', 1, 'The nine regions include right and left hypochondrium, epigastric, lumbar, umbilical, iliac and hypogastric regions.'],
  ['Which sequence represents the SOCRATES pain assessment?', 'Signs, Oxygen, Circulation, Response, Airway, Temperature, Examination, Safety|Site, Onset, Circulation, Respiration, Airway, Temperature, Exposure, Severity|Symptoms, Observations, Cause, Risk, Assessment, Treatment, Escalation, Safety|Site, Onset, Character, Radiation, Associated signs, Timescale, Exacerbating or alleviating factors, Severity', 3, 'SOCRATES covers Site, Onset, Character, Radiation, Associated signs, Timescale, Exacerbating or alleviating factors and Severity.'],
  ['What does the S in SAMPLE represent?', 'Signs and symptoms|Allergies|Medications|Past medical history', 0, 'SAMPLE begins with Signs and symptoms.'],
  ['Which information belongs under the E in SAMPLE?', 'The patient exact pain score only|The last ECG result only|Events leading to the emergency call and symptom progression|The examination of the ears', 2, 'The E covers events, including what led to the call, symptom progression and self-care.'],
  ['What should happen if the patient is time critical after the primary survey?', 'Complete every secondary-survey question before moving|Wait for all advanced testing on scene|Stop monitoring during transfer|Move to hospital with continuous assessment and treatment en route', 3, 'The deck advises continuous assessment and treatment en route when the patient is time critical.'],
  ['According to the deck, what is described as an acute abdomen?', 'Any abdominal pain lasting more than a year|Abdominal pain of less than seven days with no diagnosis after examination and investigations|Pain that is always caused by appendicitis|A confirmed diagnosis of bowel obstruction', 1, 'The deck describes an acute abdomen as less than seven days duration with no diagnosis after examination and investigations.'],
  ['What is bowel obstruction?', 'Inflammation of the appendix only|Bleeding from the stomach only|Partial or complete obstruction of the small or large intestine|A weakness in the abdominal wall', 2, 'Bowel obstruction is a partial or complete obstruction of the small or large intestine.'],
  ['Which finding may suggest bowel obstruction?', 'Faecal vomiting, abdominal distension and absence of flatus|Improved appetite with regular bowel movements|A normal passage of flatus with no abdominal pain|Mild isolated headache', 0, 'The deck lists faecal vomiting, absolute constipation and absence of flatus among possible features.'],
  ['Which is a possible cause of bowel obstruction?', 'A healed skin abrasion|Adhesions|A normal bowel movement|A simple headache', 1, 'Causes listed include adhesions, hernias, inflammatory bowel disease, tumours and diverticulitis.'],
  ['What is an abdominal aortic aneurysm?', 'Inflammation of the appendix|A blockage of the small bowel only|A tear in the stomach lining|An abnormal widening of the aorta caused by weakening of its wall', 3, 'An AAA occurs when the aortic wall weakens and stretches, increasing the risk of rupture.'],
  ['Which action is appropriate when an AAA is suspected?', 'Palpate repeatedly to confirm a pulsatile mass|Ask the patient to walk around|Avoid palpating the abdomen and escalate urgently|Delay transfer until the pain resolves', 2, 'The deck specifically states not to palpate when AAA is suspected and identifies it as a time-critical emergency.'],
  ['Which pain description may be associated with a ruptured AAA?', 'Tearing or ripping pain|Itching pain only|Pain relieved by every meal|Pain limited to the outer ear', 0, 'The deck lists tearing or ripping pain and possible pain in the chest, lower back, loin or groin.'],
  ['According to the deck, which observations should be recorded when AAA is suspected?', 'Record temperature in one foot only|Measure pain without checking vital signs|Avoid all observations until hospital|Record blood pressure and heart rate in both arms where appropriate', 3, 'The deck advises noting blood pressure and heart rate in both arms.'],
  ['What happens in an aortic dissection?', 'The appendix becomes infected|A tear allows blood to track into the wall and form a false lumen|The bowel becomes completely empty|The lungs fill with surfactant', 1, 'In dissection, blood flows through a new false lumen within the aortic wall as well as the original true lumen.'],
  ['How are gastrointestinal bleeds divided in the deck?', 'Into upper and lower GI bleeding using the duodenum as the reference point|Into arterial and venous bleeding using the appendix|Into internal and external bleeding using the stomach|Into acute and chronic bleeding using the rectum only', 0, 'The deck divides GI bleeding into upper sources above the duodenum and lower sources below it.'],
  ['Which presentation suggests an upper GI bleed?', 'Bright-red blood per rectum only|Clear urine|Haematemesis or melaena|A dry cough without blood', 2, 'Haematemesis or melaena suggests an upper GI source in the deck.'],
  ['Which presentation may suggest a lower GI bleed?', 'Coffee-ground vomit only|Blood-stained sputum only|A normal bowel movement|Bright-red or dark blood with clots passed per rectum', 3, 'The deck associates bright-red or dark blood with clots passed per rectum with a lower GI source.'],
  ['Which medication history is relevant when assessing GI bleeding?', 'Aspirin, NSAIDs, anticoagulants and antiplatelet therapy|Only moisturiser use|Only vitamin C intake|Only topical sunscreen', 0, 'The deck specifically highlights aspirin, NSAIDs, anticoagulants and antiplatelet therapy.'],
  ['Which presentation is commonly associated with appendicitis?', 'Painless swelling of both ankles only|Right lower-quadrant pain with nausea or loss of appetite|A cough with clear sputum only|Pain limited to the left shoulder in every case', 1, 'Appendicitis commonly causes right-lower-abdominal pain and may include nausea, vomiting and loss of appetite.'],
  ['What is a peptic ulcer?', 'A weakness in the abdominal wall|A blockage of the aorta|An erosion of the lining of the stomach or small intestine|An infection of the appendix only', 2, 'The deck defines a peptic ulcer as an erosion of the lining of the stomach or small intestine.'],
  ['What is diverticulosis?', 'The diverticula are always infected|The appendix is inflamed|The bowel is completely obstructed|Diverticula are present without diverticulitis or associated symptoms', 3, 'The deck distinguishes diverticulosis from diverticulitis: diverticulosis means diverticula are present without symptoms.'],
  ['Why is a strangulated hernia an emergency?', 'It always resolves without treatment|It can compromise blood supply and lead to ischaemic bowel|It only affects the skin|It prevents all abdominal pain', 1, 'The deck states that a strangulated hernia can lead to ischaemic bowel and requires urgent hospital treatment.'],
  ['Which symptom may occur with inflammatory bowel disease?', 'Diarrhoea with blood or mucus and abdominal pain|Only improved appetite|A painless fracture|A normal bowel pattern in every case', 0, 'The deck describes diarrhoea, blood or mucus in stool and abdominal pain in ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease.'],
  ['What is gastroenteritis commonly associated with?', 'Aortic rupture only|A fractured cervical vertebra|Diarrhoea and vomiting, usually from bacterial or viral causes|Permanent bowel obstruction in every case', 2, 'The deck describes gastroenteritis as commonly causing diarrhoea and vomiting and usually being bacterial or viral.'],
  ['What is advised during abdominal examination in the deck?', 'Begin with deep palpation over the most painful area|Obtain consent, consider a chaperone, inspect before auscultation and avoid palpation unless performed very lightly by a senior clinician where appropriate|Skip inspection if the patient is anxious|Palpate forcefully to locate every mass', 1, 'The deck advises consent and a chaperone, inspection, right-lower-quadrant auscultation and only very light senior-clinician palpation where appropriate.'],
  ['Which is an appropriate management and escalation principle for abdominal conditions?', 'Delay analgesia until a final diagnosis is confirmed|Stop monitoring once baseline observations are recorded|Wait until hospital arrival before escalating or pre-alerting|Correct primary-survey problems, monitor trends, provide appropriate analgesia and escalate using SBAR', 3, 'The deck emphasises primary-survey management, full history, analgesia, monitoring, SBAR escalation and pre-alert when needed.']
]);
addQuestions('cardiac-conditions', [
  ['What does coronary artery disease primarily affect?', 'The arteries supplying the myocardium|The valves in the legs|The airways in the lungs|The bones of the chest wall', 0, 'Coronary artery disease affects the arteries that supply blood to the myocardium.'],
  ['Which is a non-modifiable risk factor for coronary artery disease?', 'Smoking|Family history|Sedentary lifestyle|Excess alcohol consumption', 1, 'The deck identifies heredity and family history as non-modifiable risk factors.'],
  ['Which is a modifiable coronary artery disease risk factor?', 'Increasing age|Heredity|Smoking|Sex-related risk', 2, 'Smoking is a modifiable risk factor identified in the deck.'],
  ['What is atherosclerosis?', 'A sudden airway obstruction|A failure of the heart valves only|An infection of the myocardium|Build-up of plaque that narrows an artery', 3, 'Atherosclerosis involves plaque build-up within the arterial wall, which can narrow the vessel.'],
  ['What does complete obstruction of a coronary artery cause?', 'Infarction|Improved coronary flow|Stable blood flow at rest|Reduced risk of tissue damage', 0, 'The deck describes complete obstruction as causing infarction, or tissue death.'],
  ['When is stable angina typically provoked?', 'During sleep only|By exertion or emotional stress|Only after eating a large meal|By lying flat in every case', 1, 'Stable angina is typically provoked by exertion or emotional stress.'],
  ['Which symptom may occur with stable angina?', 'Pain limited to the lower leg|A rash on both hands|Chest tightness that may radiate to the jaw|Painless swelling of the scalp', 2, 'Stable angina may feel like chest discomfort or tightness and can radiate to the jaw, neck, arms, back or epigastrium.'],
  ['What is an appropriate initial action for a patient with symptoms consistent with angina?', 'Place the patient at rest and reduce unnecessary exertion|Ask the patient to walk to test symptoms|Delay assessment until the pain stops|Give food before taking observations', 0, 'The deck advises placing the patient at rest and reducing the workload of the heart while assessment continues.'],
  ['What is heart failure?', 'A condition in which the lungs stop filtering air|An abnormality of cardiac structure or function in which the heart cannot pump enough blood to meet the body\'s needs|A brief episode of normal exertion|A disease affecting only the skin', 1, 'The deck describes heart failure as an abnormality of cardiac structure or function in which the heart cannot deliver oxygen at the rate the body needs.'],
  ['Which relationship does the deck give for cardiac output?', 'Cardiac output = blood pressure minus pulse|Cardiac output = respiratory rate multiplied by temperature|Cardiac output = stroke volume multiplied by heart rate|Cardiac output = oxygen saturation divided by age', 2, 'The deck states that cardiac output equals stroke volume multiplied by heart rate.'],
  ['What is preload?', 'The pressure needed to eject blood from the left ventricle|The forcefulness of myocardial contraction|The oxygen level in venous blood|The volume stretching the resting heart muscle at the end of diastole', 3, 'Preload is the volume of blood stretching the resting heart muscle at the end of diastole.'],
  ['What does contractility describe?', 'The forcefulness of the heart muscle contraction|The amount of blood in the lungs|The pressure in the aorta only|The volume of air moved per minute', 0, 'Contractility is the forcefulness of myocardial contraction.'],
  ['What does afterload describe?', 'The patient\'s pain score|The pressure needed for the left ventricle to eject blood|The volume of blood returning to the right atrium|The number of coronary arteries', 1, 'Afterload is the pressure the left ventricle must overcome to eject blood around the body.'],
  ['Which group contains signs and symptoms highlighted as common in heart failure?', 'Rash, itching and ear pain|Isolated ankle pain and blurred vision|Fatigue, dyspnoea and congestion|Cough only after drinking water', 2, 'The deck highlights fatigue including exercise intolerance, dyspnoea and congestion as common features.'],
  ['What may make breathlessness worse in heart failure?', 'Wearing a watch|Sitting upright|Speaking briefly|Lying flat', 3, 'The deck specifically notes breathlessness while lying down.'],
  ['Which action is included in heart failure management?', 'Encourage repeated exertion|Place the patient at rest and obtain a full set of observations|Delay oxygen assessment until discharge|Avoid obtaining an ECG', 1, 'The management slide advises DR ABCDE, rest, a full set of observations, oxygen if required and an ECG.'],
  ['Which conditions are included within acute coronary syndrome?', 'Asthma and pneumonia|Stable hypertension and anaemia|Unstable angina, NSTEMI and STEMI|Heartburn and indigestion only', 2, 'The deck includes unstable angina, NSTEMI and STEMI within acute coronary syndrome.'],
  ['Why is acute coronary syndrome time-critical?', 'The symptoms always resolve without treatment|It only affects the skin|It cannot affect cardiac output|Ongoing ischaemia can cause permanent myocardial damage', 3, 'Cardiac muscle cannot regenerate, so ongoing ischaemia can cause permanent damage.'],
  ['Which is a common symptom of acute coronary syndrome?', 'Heavy or squeezing chest pain|Pain limited to one toe|An itchy scalp without other symptoms|Painless shoulder movement', 0, 'The deck lists heavy, squeezing, crushing or tight chest pain as a common ACS symptom.'],
  ['What is important about atypical acute coronary syndrome presentations?', 'They never occur in people with diabetes|They can include shortness of breath or collapse without chest pain|They only occur after a confirmed ECG|They rule out myocardial infarction', 1, 'The deck warns that patients may present with shortness of breath or collapse without chest pain.'],
  ['Which groups does the deck identify as having atypical ACS presentations?', 'Only patients with minor injuries|Only patients under 18|Younger adults, older adults, unwell people with diabetes and unwell female patients|Only patients with a previous fracture', 2, 'The deck identifies younger adults, older adults, unwell diabetic patients and unwell female patients as groups in which atypical presentations may be observed.'],
  ['What should be obtained as soon as possible in suspected ACS?', 'A 12-lead ECG|A dietary questionnaire|A routine hearing test|A skin allergy test', 0, 'The ACS management slide advises obtaining a 12-lead ECG as soon as possible.'],
  ['When does the supplied deck advise oxygen for suspected ACS?', 'For every patient regardless of saturation|Only after hospital arrival|When oxygen saturation is below 94% on air|Only when the patient requests it', 2, 'The deck advises oxygen when saturation is below 94% on air, within local guidance.'],
  ['Which statement best describes sickle cell crisis management?', 'Follow the patient\'s own plan when available and reassess frequently|Avoid asking about the patient\'s usual care plan|Delay pain relief until all tests are complete|Encourage strenuous exercise', 0, 'The deck advises following the patient\'s own plan when available, providing appropriate treatment and repeating assessment.'],
  ['Which finding may occur in sickle cell crisis?', 'Improved hydration|Reduced oxygen saturation|Normal breathing as the only finding|A healed minor abrasion', 1, 'The deck lists reduced oxygen saturation among findings that may occur in sickle cell crisis.'],
  ['Which factor may precipitate sickling or a sickle cell crisis?', 'Adequate hydration|Warmth alone|Dehydration|A normal rest period', 2, 'Dehydration is one of the precipitating factors listed in the deck.'],
  ['Which investigation is included in the deck\'s sickle cell crisis management plan?', 'A routine hearing test|A dietary questionnaire|A skin allergy test|A 12-lead ECG', 3, 'The sickle cell management slide includes a 12-lead ECG in the plan. This avoids ambiguity between acute chest syndrome and acute coronary syndrome.'],
  ['How should unnecessary exertion be reduced when transferring a patient in sickle cell crisis?', 'Ask the patient to walk a long distance|Keep the patient standing throughout|Move the patient without reassessing breathing|Move from chair to ambulance carefully to avoid worsening hypoxia', 3, 'The deck advises chair-to-ambulance movement to avoid exacerbating hypoxia.'],
  ['Where should a patient in sickle cell crisis usually be conveyed?', 'To any location without considering specialist care|To the patient\'s own specialist unit unless a higher-priority pathway takes precedence|Only to a minor injuries unit|Home if pain improves briefly', 1, 'The deck advises conveyance to the patient\'s own specialist unit unless another urgent pathway takes precedence.'],
  ['Which combination best summarises immediate care for suspected ACS?', 'Delay ECG, encourage walking and wait for symptoms to settle|Focus only on the patient\'s family history|Correct major ABC problems, obtain an early ECG, give treatment allowed by local clinical guidance and arrange rapid transport|Give food and postpone observations', 2, 'The deck prioritises DR ABCDE, early ECG, correction of major ABC problems, treatment permitted by local guidance, reassurance and rapid transport.']
]);
addQuestions('maxillofacial-injuries', [
  ['Which mechanism is commonly associated with maxillofacial injury?', 'Road traffic collision|A minor skin irritation|Routine exercise without impact|Uncomplicated indigestion', 0, 'The deck identifies road traffic collisions, assaults, falls and sports injuries as common causes.'],
  ['Which immediate complication can follow a maxillofacial injury?', 'Improved airway clearance|Airway compromise|Increased visual acuity|Reduced risk of aspiration', 1, 'Airway compromise is one of the immediate complications listed in the deck.'],
  ['What must be considered while managing the airway in a facial injury?', 'The patient must always be laid flat|Eye assessment must always come first|Cervical-spine injury may also need consideration, without delaying a life-saving airway intervention|Bleeding can be ignored until discharge', 2, 'Consider cervical-spine injury, but do not delay clearing and managing a compromised airway.'],
  ['Which protection is especially important when managing an unconscious patient with facial injuries?', 'Hearing and ankle protection only|No PPE if bleeding is present|Facial and eye protection|A dry dressing over the mouth', 2, 'The deck specifically highlights facial and eye protection.'],
  ['When there is no overriding cervical-spine concern, how are patients with facial injuries generally positioned?', 'Flat on the back with the head extended|Standing unsupported|Prone with the face pressed down|Sitting up and leaning forward', 3, 'Sitting up and leaning forward can allow blood and debris to drain from the mouth, but spinal concerns may override this position.'],
  ['What should happen if a facial injury causes a non-patent airway?', 'Wait for the swelling to settle|Clear debris and manage the airway while requesting appropriate help|Cover the mouth with a dressing|Complete a full secondary survey first', 1, 'The deck stresses that a lack of a patent airway can kill the patient first and debris should be cleared.'],
  ['What does a blowout fracture affect?', 'The lower leg only|The outer ear only|The orbit around the eye|The abdominal wall', 2, 'A blow to the eye can cause the orbit to blow out and fracture.'],
  ['Which finding may indicate an orbital blowout injury?', 'Improved upward vision|Enophthalmos, where the eyeball retracts into the socket|A normal pupil in every case|Pain only in the ankle', 1, 'The deck lists enophthalmos as a sign of blowout fracture.'],
  ['Why are avulsed teeth important in an unconscious facial-injury patient?', 'They always improve breathing|They prevent aspiration|They only affect long-term appearance|They can be inhaled and cause airway obstruction', 3, 'The deck warns that avulsed teeth can be inhaled in an unconscious patient.'],
  ['Why can facial lacerations appear especially dramatic?', 'Facial tissue has an abundant blood supply|Facial tissue cannot bleed|All facial wounds involve major arteries|The skin always splits through bone', 0, 'The rich blood supply of facial tissue can make wounds look dramatic.'],
  ['What should be done when a laceration passes completely through the cheek?', 'Apply pressure only to the patient\'s forehead|Apply pressure to both sides of the wound|Avoid controlling the bleeding|Pack the ear canal', 1, 'The deck states that pressure may be needed on both sides of a through-and-through cheek wound.'],
  ['What is the first priority when a chemical enters the eye?', 'Apply direct pressure to the eye|Protect yourself and begin copious irrigation with clean water|Cover the unaffected eye and delay irrigation|Try to neutralise the chemical with another substance', 1, 'Chemical eye injuries require PPE and prompt copious irrigation with clean water.'],
  ['What is the appropriate duration for initial irrigation of a chemical eye injury?', 'Ten seconds only|Until the patient stops speaking|At least 20 minutes, continuing according to pH and local guidance|Only after specialist assessment', 2, 'Irrigate immediately for at least 20 minutes and continue according to pH and local clinical guidance.'],
  ['What should be avoided during irrigation of a chemical eye injury?', 'Protective eyewear|Clean water|Contaminating the unaffected eye|Continuous reassessment', 2, 'The deck specifically warns not to contaminate the unaffected eye.'],
  ['What is a corneal abrasion?', 'A fracture of the maxilla|A ruptured eardrum|A deep injury to the femur|A superficial injury to the surface of the cornea', 3, 'The deck defines a corneal abrasion as a superficial injury to the corneal surface.'],
  ['What is appropriate when assessing blunt eye trauma?', 'Check visual acuity in each eye separately|Assume normal vision excludes serious injury|Apply direct pressure to the eye|Remove any object embedded in the globe', 0, 'The deck advises assessing visual acuity and examining the patient even when vision appears unaffected.'],
  ['What should be done with an object embedded in the cornea or inside the eye?', 'Remove it with a cotton swab|Push it further into the eye|Do not remove it and convey the patient to hospital|Rinse it out forcefully', 2, 'Embedded or intra-ocular foreign bodies should not be removed and require hospital care.'],
  ['How should dressings be applied around an embedded eye object?', 'Press directly on the object|Build them up around the object to prevent movement|Cover only the unaffected eye|Wrap the dressing tightly around the neck', 1, 'The deck advises built-up dressings around the object and no direct pressure on the eye.'],
  ['Which eye assessment should be documented?', 'Only the colour of the patient\'s clothing|Only the patient\'s blood group|Pupil size, shape, equality and reaction|Only whether the eye is painful', 2, 'The deck advises documenting pupil reaction, visual acuity and eye movement, along with the examination findings.'],
  ['If a patient with an uncomplicated eye injury is wearing contact lenses, what should be done when safe?', 'Leave them in place in every case|Push them under the upper eyelid|Remove them with a sharp instrument|Ask the patient to remove them if possible', 3, 'Check for contact lenses and ask the patient to remove them if possible, but do not manipulate a penetrating or embedded eye injury.'],
  ['How should a patient with a nosebleed initially be positioned?', 'Sitting down and leaning forward|Lying flat with the head tilted back|Standing with the head held backwards|Prone with the face down', 0, 'The deck advises sitting the patient down and leaning forward.'],
  ['How long should the patient initially squeeze the soft part of the nose?', 'Ten seconds|One minute|Ten minutes|Until the patient falls asleep', 2, 'The deck advises squeezing the soft part of the nose for ten minutes before reassessment.'],
  ['What should the patient do with blood during a nosebleed?', 'Swallow all blood quickly|Blow the nose repeatedly|Gently spit it out rather than swallowing it|Lie flat and allow blood to collect', 2, 'The deck advises breathing through the mouth and gently spitting blood out rather than swallowing it.'],
  ['Which finding should prompt transport or a lower threshold for assessment in a patient with epistaxis?', 'Only when bleeding lasts more than ten seconds|Only if the patient has no medication history|After every nosebleed regardless of duration|Bleeding continues for more than 30 minutes, or anticoagulant use increases concern', 3, 'The deck highlights bleeding over 30 minutes and anticoagulant medication as reasons for transport or increased concern.'],
  ['Which finding may indicate a significant ear injury after facial or head trauma?', 'Clear fluid or blood from the ear|Improved hearing in both ears|A normal ear examination only|A painless healed scar', 0, 'The deck lists bleeding or clear fluid, potentially cerebrospinal fluid, as a concerning ear finding.'],
  ['What is the safest immediate approach for suspected eardrum rupture or ear drainage?', 'Put liquid into the ear to clean it|Probe the canal to find the rupture|Cover the outer ear with a loose sterile dressing, avoid inserting anything and convey the patient to hospital|Block the canal tightly with a dressing', 2, 'Do not put liquid into or probe the ear. Cover it externally without blocking drainage and arrange hospital assessment.'],
  ['How should a cut on the outer ear initially be managed?', 'Apply direct pressure until bleeding stops|Wash deeply inside the ear canal|Apply pressure to the eyeball|Leave the wound uncovered and unobserved', 0, 'The deck advises direct pressure followed by a sterile dressing shaped to the ear.'],
  ['What should be done if part of the outer ear has been amputated?', 'Discard it because it cannot be used|Put it inside the ear canal|Apply direct pressure to the amputated part only|Wrap or seal it cleanly, keep it cool without direct ice contact and send it with the patient', 3, 'Keep the amputated part, protect it in a clean bag or wrap and cool it without placing it directly on ice.'],
  ['What should not be done when there is drainage from inside the ear?', 'Cover the outside with a loose sterile dressing|Consider cervical-spine injury|Convey the patient to hospital|Block the drainage or clean the ear canal', 3, 'The deck specifically says not to block drainage or clean and wash the inside of the ear canal.'],
  ['Which action best summarises safe management of a serious maxillofacial injury?', 'Prioritise airway and bleeding control, protect injured eyes and arrange early specialist help|Focus only on the appearance of the wound|Remove all embedded objects before transport|Delay assessment until facial swelling has resolved', 0, 'The deck prioritises <C>ABC management, airway support, bleeding control, eye protection and early advanced assistance when required.']
]);
addQuestions('post-operative-complications', [
  ['When can post-operative complications typically begin according to the deck?', 'From 1 day after surgery onwards, including 5 or more days|Only during the operation itself|Only after six months|Only before the patient leaves theatre', 0, 'The deck states that post-operative complications typically occur from 1 day after surgery through 5 or more days afterwards.'],
  ['Which broad timing groups does the deck use to organise post-operative complications?', 'Days 1, 10 and 30 only|Days 0-2, days 3-5 and 5 or more days after surgery|Only the first 24 hours|Only complications after day 14', 1, 'The deck uses early, intermediate and later timing groups. The bands guide assessment and are not diagnostic cut-offs.'],
  ['How can post-operative complications present?', 'Only as a wound problem|Only as a fever|As a complication specific to the operation or as more general illness|Only as a problem with mobility', 2, 'The deck states that complications may be specific to the surgery or more generalised in presentation.'],
  ['Which group contains complications highlighted in the deck?', 'Headache, rash and ankle pain|Only pain at the surgical site|Only urinary symptoms|Fever, wound infections, venous thromboembolic events and deep vein thrombosis', 3, 'The deck highlights fever, wound infections, embolic complications and deep vein thrombosis as common complications.'],
  ['Which complications are highlighted in the 0-2 day period?', 'Post-operative fever, surgery-specific infection and haematoma|Urinary tract infection and pulmonary embolism only|Only chronic pain|Only late wound infection', 0, 'The deck lists post-operative fever, specific infection relating to the surgery and haematoma in days 0-2.'],
  ['What should post-operative fever be treated as initially?', 'A confirmed diagnosis of wound infection|A clinical finding requiring assessment and differential diagnosis|Proof that the operation has failed|A reason to ignore other observations', 1, 'Post-operative fever is a finding that requires assessment, observations and senior clinical differential diagnosis.'],
  ['What is a haematoma after surgery?', 'A type of urinary infection|A respiratory complication only|A collection of blood that may cause swelling, bruising or pain|A normal observation after every operation', 2, 'A haematoma is a collection of blood and may cause local swelling, bruising, pain or pressure.'],
  ['Which complication is listed in the deck for days 3-5 after surgery?', 'Only a minor headache|Only a healed wound|Only chronic pain|Pneumonia', 3, 'Pneumonia is listed among the days 3-5 complications.'],
  ['Why is sepsis important in a post-operative patient?', 'It is a serious possible complication that requires early recognition and escalation|It always resolves without treatment|It only affects the wound surface|It can be excluded by a single normal temperature', 0, 'The deck identifies sepsis as a serious post-operative complication and advises early escalation when the patient is unwell.'],
  ['When can wound infection occur according to the deck?', 'Only during the operation|In both early and later post-operative periods|Only after one year|Only when there is no pain', 1, 'Wound infection appears in both the days 3-5 and 5 or more day groups.'],
  ['In which time periods is deep vein thrombosis listed?', 'Only before surgery|Only during days 0-1|Days 3-5 and 5 or more days after surgery|Only after discharge from hospital', 2, 'The deck lists deep vein thrombosis in both the days 3-5 and 5 or more day groups.'],
  ['What is phlebitis?', 'A lung infection|A collection of blood in the wound|A urinary complication|Inflammation of a vein, which may cause local pain, redness or tenderness', 3, 'Phlebitis is inflammation of a vein and may cause local pain, redness, tenderness or a palpable tender vein.'],
  ['Which complication is listed in the deck from day 5 onward?', 'Urinary tract infection|Only an immediate anaesthetic reaction|Only a surgical haematoma|Only a pre-operative problem', 0, 'Urinary tract infection is included in the day 5 or later group.'],
  ['Which respiratory problem is included among later post-operative complications?', 'Improved exercise tolerance|Chest infection|A healed wound|A minor finger injury', 1, 'Chest infection is listed among the complications occurring 5 or more days after surgery.'],
  ['Why is pulmonary embolism a serious post-operative concern?', 'It is always harmless|It only causes local skin changes|It can cause sudden breathlessness, chest pain, reduced oxygen saturation or collapse|It can be ruled out by the operation date alone', 2, 'The deck lists pulmonary embolism as a later complication and highlights sudden respiratory or circulatory deterioration as concerning.'],
  ['How should the timing bands in the deck be used?', 'As fixed rules that exclude complications outside the band|As a replacement for clinical assessment|As proof of the diagnosis|As a guide to questions and differential diagnosis, not as a diagnostic rule', 3, 'Timing helps guide assessment but does not exclude a complication outside the usual period or establish a diagnosis.'],
  ['What does the management slide specifically require?', 'A full set of observations|Only a wound photograph|Only a pain score|No observations if the patient is awake', 0, 'The deck instructs clinicians to complete a full set of observations.'],
  ['Who should complete the differential diagnosis?', 'The receiving team without a clinical handover|The senior clinician|The patient\'s relative|The patient alone', 1, 'The deck states that the senior clinician should complete a differential diagnosis from the presenting findings.'],
  ['Which information is important in a post-operative handover?', 'Only the patient\'s age|Only the operation theatre number|The operation and date, new complaint, observations, findings and treatment given|Only whether the wound is covered', 2, 'A useful handover includes the operation and date, symptoms, findings, observations, treatments and clinical concern.'],
  ['Why should assessment extend beyond the surgical site?', 'The patient has no wound symptoms|General complications only occur before surgery|The surgical site is never relevant|A local wound problem can coexist with a general complication', 3, 'A local complaint can coexist with pneumonia, sepsis, thrombosis or another general complication.'],
  ['Which finding should prompt urgent escalation?', 'Worsening breathlessness, reduced oxygen saturation or collapse|A stable observation set with improving symptoms|A healed scar|A normal appetite by itself', 0, 'Worsening breathing, hypoxia, collapse and other signs of deterioration are red flags requiring urgent escalation.'],
  ['Who should identify the appropriate patient care pathway?', 'The patient alone without clinical review|The senior clinician|The receiving service without the patient assessment|The patient\'s employer', 1, 'The management slide states that the senior clinician should identify the appropriate patient care pathway.'],
  ['When may conveyance to the nearest emergency department be appropriate?', 'Only when the patient requests it|Only after every possible diagnosis is confirmed|When the patient is acutely unwell or cannot be safely managed in the current setting|Never after surgery', 2, 'The deck advises conveyance to the nearest emergency department if appropriate. Clinical need, patient stability and available specialist pathways should guide the decision.'],
  ['What should happen if the patient is deteriorating before the diagnosis is complete?', 'Wait for a complete diagnosis before escalating|Discharge the patient if the wound looks normal|Focus only on the operation date|Escalate and treat immediate threats while continuing assessment', 3, 'Do not delay escalation while trying to establish a complete diagnosis in a deteriorating patient.'],
  ['Which history item may increase concern in a post-operative patient with a possible clotting complication?', 'Current anticoagulant medication, a previous clotting problem or reduced mobility|A preference for a particular meal|A healed childhood scar|The patient\'s shoe size', 0, 'Ask about anticoagulants, previous clotting problems, mobility and new limb or respiratory symptoms.'],
  ['Why should observations be repeated?', 'A single normal set always rules out complications|The patient can deteriorate and trends may reveal a developing complication|Repeated observations are never useful|Only the temperature can change after surgery', 1, 'Repeat observations and review their trend because post-operative patients can deteriorate and complications may overlap.'],
  ['Which presentation may suggest deep vein thrombosis?', 'Improved movement in both legs|A normal limb with no symptoms|Unilateral limb pain, swelling or warmth|A healed surgical wound with no other symptoms', 2, 'New unilateral limb pain, swelling, warmth or a change in circumference may indicate DVT and requires assessment.'],
  ['Which presentation may suggest pulmonary embolism?', 'A stable wound with no symptoms|Improved appetite only|A minor bruise at the operation site|Sudden or worsening breathlessness, chest pain, hypoxia, syncope or unexplained deterioration', 3, 'The learn material identifies sudden respiratory or circulatory change as concerning for pulmonary embolism.'],
  ['What should be considered when assessing post-operative fever?', 'The time since surgery, operation, wound, respiratory and urinary symptoms, medications and observations|Only the temperature reading|Only whether the patient has pain|Only the appearance of the dressing', 0, 'Fever requires assessment of timing, operation, symptoms, observations, medications and possible sources of infection.'],
  ['Which statement best summarises the management of post-operative complications?', 'Use timing alone to diagnose the problem|Complete full observations, involve a senior clinician in the differential diagnosis and choose the appropriate pathway|Focus only on the wound|Wait until day 5 before escalating', 1, 'The deck management approach is full observations, senior-clinician differential diagnosis and selection of the appropriate care pathway or emergency department transfer.']
]);
addQuestions('burns', [
  ['What is a burn?', 'An injury caused only by blunt trauma|A disease affecting the digestive system|An injury caused by exposure to energy|A wound caused only by cold weather', 2, 'The deck defines a burn as an injury caused by exposure to energy such as thermal, electrical, chemical or radiation energy.'],
  ['What is a scald?', 'A burn caused by hot liquid or steam|A burn caused only by an electrical current|A burn caused only by radiation|A burn caused by a blunt impact', 0, 'A scald is a burn caused by contact with hot liquid or steam.'],
  ['Which is a cause of a burn identified in the deck?', 'Only low blood sugar|Only a ligament injury|Only dehydration|Chemical exposure', 3, 'The deck identifies thermal, electrical, chemical and radiation energy as causes of burns.'],
  ['Which type of injury occurs when electricity flows through the body from an entry point to an exit point?', 'A superficial scald|An electrical burn|A pressure ulcer|A radiation-only injury', 1, 'Electrical burns occur when electricity flows through the body from an entry point to an exit point.'],
  ['What may make a chemical burn particularly hazardous?', 'Contact with a non-corrosive warm surface|Contact with corrosive agents such as acids, alkalis or cement|Brief exposure to warm air|Contact with a clean dry dressing', 1, 'Chemical burns can result from corrosive acids, alkalis, bleaches, cement and some organic products.'],
  ['Which feature is associated with a superficial or first-degree burn?', 'Deep tissue destruction with no sensation in every case|A charred appearance through all dermal layers|A permanent cavity|Redness and pain without blistering', 3, 'Superficial burns involve the epidermis, cause redness and pain, and do not blister in the deck description.'],
  ['Which feature is associated with a partial-thickness burn?', 'Blistering, significant pain and a moist appearance|No pain and a waxen appearance in every case|Only intact, uninjured epidermis|A projectile entry wound', 0, 'Partial-thickness burns involve the epidermis and dermis and may blister, hurt and appear moist.'],
  ['Which feature may indicate a full-thickness burn?', 'Red skin that always heals without scarring|A moist surface with normal sensation only|May appear white, waxen or charred and non-blanching|A small bruise without skin change', 2, 'Full-thickness burns may appear white, waxen or charred, are non-blanching and can be painless because nerves are destroyed.'],
  ['Why may burn colour be an unreliable guide to depth?', 'Colour always identifies the exact depth|Burn depth never changes over time|Only the patient age affects colour|Skin pigmentation can make redness or full-thickness change harder to recognise', 3, 'The deck warns that pigmentation can obscure superficial redness or make full-thickness burns resemble unburnt skin.'],
  ['Which is a burn red flag requiring early escalation?', 'A small painless bruise away from the burn|Airway burns, soot or respiratory distress|A normal respiratory rate with no exposure history|A minor superficial burn on an uninjured area', 1, 'Airway burns, soot, oedema, respiratory distress and hot-gas inhalation are red flags.'],
  ['Which burn location or pattern is a red flag?', 'A small isolated superficial burn with no symptoms|A healed scar|A circumferential burn of the chest, neck or limb|A minor area of redness only', 2, 'Circumferential burns of the chest, neck or a limb can compromise breathing or circulation.'],
  ['Which TBSA threshold does the deck highlight as a burn red flag in adults?', 'More than 15 percent TBSA|More than 1 percent TBSA in every case|More than 50 percent TBSA only|More than 85 percent TBSA only', 0, 'The deck highlights burns above 15 percent TBSA in adults.'],
  ['Which TBSA threshold does the deck highlight as a burn red flag in children?', 'More than 1 percent TBSA in every case|More than 10 percent TBSA|More than 50 percent TBSA only|More than 90 percent TBSA only', 1, 'The deck highlights burns above 10 percent TBSA in children.'],
  ['What should be considered with a possible inhalation injury?', 'Only a minor ankle injury|Improved voice and normal breathing in every case|A healed surgical scar|Airway oedema, stridor, black sputum, wheeze or hoarseness', 3, 'The deck lists airway burns, soot, inhalation injury, stridor, black sputum, wheezing and hoarseness as concerning findings.'],
  ['Which primary-survey approach is used in the supplied burns deck?', 'Delay the primary survey until the burn percentage is calculated|Assess only the skin and ignore breathing|Complete DR CAcBCDE and correct problems as they are found|Complete a full social history before airway assessment', 2, 'The deck uses DR CAcBCDE and advises addressing problems as they are found.'],
  ['What history is particularly relevant after a burn?', 'Time, mechanism, contact duration, cooling provided and environment|Only the patient favourite food|Only the patient shoe size|Only the patient postcode', 0, 'The secondary-survey history includes time, source, contact duration, treatment or cooling, mechanism and environment.'],
  ['What is one reason to complete a secondary survey in a burn patient?', 'To replace the primary survey|To avoid taking observations|To decide that no transfer is ever needed|To identify associated injuries, co-morbidities and safeguarding concerns', 3, 'The secondary survey looks for other injuries, co-morbidities, vulnerabilities and safeguarding issues.'],
  ['What maximum cooling period is stated in the deck for a burn?', 'Until the patient becomes hypothermic|Up to 20 minutes|For only 10 seconds|Until all pain has permanently stopped', 1, 'The deck advises cooling the burn for up to 20 minutes.'],
  ['What should be used to cool a burn when available?', 'Running water|Ice directly on the wound|Hot oil|A dry towel only', 0, 'The deck advises running water, with saline if water is not available.'],
  ['What temperature should be avoided when cooling a burn?', 'Tepid or lukewarm water|Clean running water|Ice-cold water|Saline when water is unavailable', 2, 'The deck advises against ice-cold water and recommends tepid or lukewarm water.'],
['What does \'cool the burn, not the patient\' mean?', 'Cover the patient in ice|Stop monitoring the patient during cooling|Cool the whole patient until shivering begins|Prevent ongoing burn injury while avoiding hypothermia', 3, 'Cooling should treat the burn without causing hypothermia, particularly in children and patients with extensive burns.'],
  ['After cooling, what is the correct approach to cling film according to the deck?', 'Discard the first length and apply sheets in a patchwork without wrapping circumferentially|Wrap it tightly around the whole limb|Apply it before cooling|Use it on chemical burns instead of irrigation', 0, 'The deck advises discarding the first length, applying patchwork sheets and avoiding circumferential wrapping.'],
  ['What should not be applied to a burn as routine first aid?', 'Running water|Ointments or creams|A safe temporary dressing|Appropriate analgesia', 1, 'The deck advises not putting ointments or creams onto the burn or scald.'],
  ['What should be done with constrictive items such as jewellery?', 'Leave them in place until discharge|Cover them tightly with cling film|Remove them because swelling may make later removal difficult|Use them to estimate TBSA', 2, 'The deck advises removing constrictive items and jewellery before swelling develops.'],
  ['Which method can be used to estimate TBSA for a small or patchy burn?', 'A full-body percentage estimated only from pain score|The total length of the wound in centimetres|A visual estimate that ignores patchy areas|The patient palmar surface including the fingers, approximately 1 percent', 3, 'The patient palmar surface including the fingers is used as an estimate of approximately 1 percent TBSA.'],
  ['Which value is assigned to each lower limb in Wallace Rule of Nines?', '1 percent|18 percent|9 percent|36 percent', 1, 'Wallace Rule of Nines assigns 18 percent to each lower limb.'],
  ['Which value is assigned to the genitals in Wallace Rule of Nines?', '1 percent|9 percent|18 percent|27 percent', 0, 'The deck assigns 1 percent to the genitals.'],
  ['What is important after an electrical injury?', 'Immediate discharge if the skin wound is small|Assessment of the burn only with no cardiac monitoring|A 12-lead ECG and monitoring because arrhythmias may develop|Cooling the patient in ice water', 2, 'The deck states that 12-lead monitoring is essential after electrical injury because arrhythmias may develop.'],
  ['What types of injury can follow a successful Taser deployment?', 'Thermal injury only, with no possible cardiac effects|Electrical injury and penetrating injury if the skin is broken|Respiratory injury only|Blunt injury only', 1, 'The deck describes a successful Taser deployment as electrical injury and possible penetrating injury if the skin is broken.'],
  ['What is the key management principle for a burn patient?', 'Delay analgesia until a final diagnosis is confirmed|Estimate TBSA but omit the primary survey|Cool the entire patient until shivering begins|Complete the primary survey, cool the burn safely, assess TBSA and convey to definitive care', 3, 'The deck summary emphasises primary survey, cooling, TBSA assessment, appropriate dressing, escalation and definitive care.']
]);
addQuestions('respiratory-conditions', [
  ['Which approach should be used first when assessing a patient with a respiratory complaint?', 'A structured primary survey followed by a secondary survey|A detailed social history before checking the airway|A peak-flow reading before looking at the patient|A diagnosis based only on the presenting symptom', 0, 'The deck prioritises a primary survey, followed by history, examination and observations.'],
  ['Which observation directly assesses breathing in a patient with respiratory difficulty?', 'Blood group|Respiratory rate, rhythm and depth|Visual acuity|Urine colour', 1, 'Respiratory rate, rhythm and depth directly assess the breathing pattern.'],
  ['When inspecting the chest, what should be assessed?', 'Only the patient\'s height|Only the heart rhythm|Chest shape and equal rise and fall|Only the temperature', 2, 'Inspection includes chest shape, equal chest movement, skin changes, injuries and work of breathing.'],
  ['What should be assessed during chest palpation?', 'Only the radial pulse|Only the patient\'s ability to speak|Only the skin temperature of the hands|Bony landmarks, tenderness, chest movement and subcutaneous emphysema', 3, 'The deck advises palpating the whole chest for landmarks, tenderness, movement and subcutaneous emphysema.'],
  ['How is acute breathlessness defined by its speed of onset?', 'Developing over weeks or months|Developing over hours or days|Developing suddenly or within minutes|Present only during sleep', 2, 'Acute breathlessness develops suddenly or within minutes.'],
  ['Which two conditions are identified as the main types of chest infection?', 'Asthma and COPD|Bronchitis and pneumonia|Pulmonary embolism and pneumothorax|Heart failure and angina', 1, 'The deck identifies bronchitis and pneumonia as the main types of chest infection.'],
  ['Which patient belongs to a high-risk group for chest infection?', 'A healthy adult with no risk factors|A patient with a healed minor wound|A patient with normal exercise tolerance|A patient with COPD or a weakened immune system', 3, 'COPD and weakened immunity are identified risk factors.'],
  ['Which symptom may occur with a chest infection?', 'Improved hearing|Isolated ankle pain|Persistent cough with yellow or green sputum|Increased visual acuity', 2, 'Persistent cough and coloured sputum are listed symptoms of chest infection.'],
  ['What is consolidation in pneumonia?', 'Air trapped in the pleural space|A narrowing of the coronary arteries|A fracture of the sternum|A build-up of fluid and blood cells in affected lung tissue', 3, 'The deck describes consolidation as a build-up of fluid and blood cells following inflammation.'],
  ['Which auscultation finding may support pneumonia?', 'A completely normal finding in every case|Only a loud radial pulse|A change in pupil colour|Crackles or bronchial breath sounds', 3, 'Pneumonia may cause added sounds such as crackles or bronchial breath sounds.'],
  ['Which finding in a patient with pneumonia requires urgent escalation?', 'A normal respiratory rate and improving symptoms|A healed scar|A single mild cough with no other findings|Severe respiratory compromise, hypoxia or significant deterioration', 3, 'Severe respiratory compromise, hypoxia and deterioration are concerning features requiring urgent escalation.'],
  ['What is a pulmonary embolism?', 'An infection of the outer ear|An obstruction of pulmonary vessels|A fracture of the ribs|A chronic skin condition', 1, 'The deck defines PE as an obstruction of the pulmonary vessels.'],
  ['Which symptom is commonly associated with pulmonary embolism?', 'Improved exercise tolerance|Only a painless skin rash|Pleuritic chest pain and unexplained tachycardia|Only a sore throat', 2, 'Pleuritic pain and unexplained tachycardia are listed PE features.'],
  ['Which is a risk factor for pulmonary embolism?', 'A normal ECG with no symptoms|A healed superficial abrasion|Regular hydration with no illness|Recent surgery or immobility', 3, 'Recent surgery and immobility are among the risk factors listed in the deck.'],
  ['Which lower-limb finding may support a suspected DVT, which can be associated with PE?', 'Equal painless legs with no change|A normal hand grip|Bilateral normal skin colour only|Unilateral swelling, warmth or calf tenderness', 3, 'Unilateral swelling, warmth, colour change or tenderness may indicate DVT; DVT can be associated with pulmonary embolism.'],
  ['Which finding is a time-critical feature in suspected PE?', 'A normal oxygen saturation with no symptoms|A mild headache only|Severe hypoxia that remains below 90% despite oxygen|A healed surgical scar', 2, 'The deck identifies severe hypoxia below 90% unresponsive to oxygen as time-critical.'],
  ['Which initial approach is appropriate for a patient with suspected PE?', 'Ask the patient to walk around to test symptoms|Delay all observations until hospital|Give treatment without checking local guidance|Maintain ABCDs, position for comfort, monitor continuously and follow JRCALC oxygen guidance', 3, 'The deck advises ABCD management, a comfortable position, baseline observations, continuous monitoring and JRCALC-guided oxygen.'],
  ['What is COPD?', 'A chronic progressive disorder characterised by airflow obstruction|A sudden fracture of the airway|A condition affecting only the skin|A type of acute eye injury', 0, 'The deck defines COPD as a chronic progressive disorder characterised by airflow obstruction.'],
  ['Which history in a patient over 35 with a relevant risk factor may support clinical suspicion of COPD?', 'Persistent progressive breathlessness, chronic cough or regular sputum|Only a single episode of fainting|Only a recent minor bruise|Only an isolated sore throat', 0, 'The deck highlights age over 35, a risk factor, persistent breathlessness, chronic cough, sputum, recurrent infections and wheeze.'],
  ['What characterises emphysema?', 'Distension and destruction of alveoli with loss of lung elasticity|A collection of blood in the ear|Inflammation of the skin only|A blockage of the coronary arteries only', 0, 'Emphysema involves alveolar destruction and loss of elastic recoil.'],
  ['Which feature may be seen in emphysema?', 'Pursed-lip expiration or a barrel-shaped chest|A silent abdomen only|A fixed dilated pupil in every case|A normal respiratory effort in every case', 0, 'Pursed-lip expiration and a barrel-shaped chest are described features.'],
  ['What target saturation range does the deck give when titrating oxygen for an appropriate COPD patient?', '70-75%|80-85%|88-92%|98-100% in every patient', 2, 'The deck gives a target saturation range of 88-92% for relevant COPD patients.'],
  ['In the COPD guidance, how long should oxygen-driven nebulisation initially be limited to?', 'Six minutes, with a further six minutes only if clinically indicated|Thirty minutes in every case|One hour before reassessment|It should always continue without review', 0, 'The deck advises limiting oxygen-driven nebulisation to six minutes and considering a further six only when clinically indicated.'],
  ['Which combination describes mechanisms contributing to asthma symptoms?', 'Bronchial inflammation, muscle contraction and increased mucus production|Only damage to the ribs|Only a pulmonary embolus|Only low blood glucose', 0, 'Asthma involves inflamed sensitive airways, bronchospasm and excess mucus.'],
  ['Which is a recognised asthma trigger?', 'Cold weather, exertion, allergy or respiratory infection|A healed fracture only|Normal hydration|A normal ECG by itself', 0, 'The deck lists respiratory infections, allergy, cold weather and exertion among common triggers.'],
  ['Which history item indicates increased risk of severe asthma?', 'Previous intensive-care admission and the patient\'s usual response to medication|Only the patient shoe size|Only the patient preferred food|Only the time of the last meal', 0, 'Previous intensive-care admission and the patient\'s usual response help assess risk and severity.'],
  ['What does peak expiratory flow rate measure?', 'The degree of airway obstruction during forced exhalation|The patient blood glucose level|The depth of a skin wound|The strength of the radial pulse', 0, 'PEFR is a rapid measure of obstruction during forced exhalation.'],
  ['How should a peak-flow reading be recorded?', 'Repeat three times and record the highest reading|Take one reading only and discard it|Record the lowest reading without repeating|Use the reading only if the patient is unconscious', 0, 'The deck advises three attempts and recording the highest result.'],
  ['Which finding indicates life-threatening asthma?', 'A silent chest, exhaustion or altered consciousness|Mild wheeze with normal speech only|A normal peak flow with no symptoms|A healed cough from last year', 0, 'Life-threatening features include a silent chest, exhaustion and altered consciousness.'],
  ['Why should hyperventilation not automatically be attributed to anxiety?', 'It may be caused by serious conditions such as PE, myocardial infarction, DKA, asthma or sepsis|It is always caused by anxiety|It never occurs in children|It can be diagnosed without checking observations', 0, 'The deck stresses that hyperventilation may reflect serious underlying disease and should not automatically be labelled anxiety.']
]);
addQuestions('abdominal-injuries', [
  ['Which three anatomical areas are identified in the abdominal-injuries deck?', 'Abdominal cavity, pelvis and retroperitoneal area|Chest, neck and pelvis|Skull, thorax and abdomen|Small bowel, spleen and liver', 0, 'The deck identifies the abdominal cavity, pelvis and retroperitoneal area.'],
  ['Which structures are found in the retroperitoneal area?', 'The lungs, heart and trachea|The kidneys, ureters, pancreas, abdominal aorta, vena cava and part of the duodenum|The brain, spinal cord and oesophagus|Only the spleen and liver', 1, 'The retroperitoneal area contains the kidneys, ureters, pancreas, abdominal aorta, vena cava and part of the duodenum.'],
  ['Which statement about blunt abdominal injury is correct?', 'It always causes an open wound|It only follows a stab wound|It is the most common pattern described and may result from direct blows or rapid deceleration|It cannot be caused by a blast', 2, 'The deck describes blunt injury as the most common pattern and includes direct blows, rapid deceleration and blast injuries.'],
  ['Which is an example of a mechanism that may cause blunt abdominal trauma?', 'A clean surgical incision|A minor skin abrasion|A superficial pressure sore|A road traffic collision or blast shockwave', 3, 'Road traffic collisions and blast shockwaves are listed causes of blunt abdominal trauma.'],
  ['Which organ is specifically identified as vulnerable in blunt abdominal trauma?', 'The spleen|The brain|The eye|The external ear', 0, 'The spleen is one of the structures highlighted as vulnerable in blunt abdominal trauma.'],
  ['Which complication may result from blunt abdominal trauma?', 'Only a superficial bruise|Internal bleeding or a ruptured organ|Only an uncomplicated skin wound|Improved circulation', 1, 'The deck lists internal bleeding and ruptured organs among blunt-trauma complications.'],
  ['What does penetrating abdominal trauma usually produce?', 'A closed injury with no contamination|Only a surface bruise|An open abdominal injury that may affect adjacent structures|A pressure ulcer', 2, 'Penetrating trauma results in an open abdominal injury and may damage adjacent structures such as the chest.'],
  ['Which structures may be injured by penetrating abdominal trauma, including when the injury crosses into the chest?', 'The spleen only|The liver only|The small bowel only|The lungs or heart, as well as abdominal structures', 3, 'The deck identifies the lungs and heart, as well as abdominal structures, as possible sites of injury.'],
  ['Which is a possible cause of penetrating abdominal trauma?', 'Gunshot wound or blast material|A normal seat-belt mark with no force|A minor graze|A pressure ulcer', 0, 'Gunshot wounds and blast material or shrapnel are listed causes of penetrating trauma.'],
  ['What should be done with a penetrating object that remains in situ?', 'Remove it before transport|Secure it before transport and avoid removing it|Probe the wound around it|Push it further into the body', 1, 'The deck says not to remove an object in situ and to secure it before transport.'],
  ['What is evisceration?', 'Bleeding from a vein|A closed abdominal bruise|Protrusion of abdominal organs outside the body|A fracture of the pelvis', 2, 'Evisceration is the protrusion of abdominal organs outside the body.'],
  ['What should be done when abdominal organs are protruding through a wound?', 'Push them back into the abdominal cavity|Scrub them with antiseptic|Leave them exposed without a dressing|Apply an appropriate dressing and do not replace the organs', 3, 'The deck advises not pushing protruding organs back and applying an appropriate dressing.'],
  ['What is the first safety priority at a potentially dangerous abdominal-trauma scene?', 'Protect personal safety and follow police or incident-command confirmation that the scene is safe|Approach the patient before checking hazards|Remove all equipment from the ambulance|Ask the patient to walk to safety', 0, 'The deck stresses personal safety and states that police should declare the scene safe.'],
  ['When assessing a stabbing mechanism, which information is particularly relevant?', 'The patient favourite meal|The length of the penetrating object, angle of insertion and whether it entered the thoracic cavity|Only the patient shoe size|Only the time of day', 1, 'The deck identifies the length of the object, insertion angle and possible thoracic-cavity entry as important.'],
  ['Which information is relevant when assessing a road traffic collision mechanism?', 'Only the patient age|Only the colour of the vehicle|Impact speed, seat-belt use and vehicle damage|Only the weather', 2, 'The deck highlights impact speed, restraint use and vehicle damage.'],
  ['What should be assessed first in the initial abdominal-trauma assessment?', 'Only the patient pain score|Only the abdomen|Only the mechanism|Complete a C-ABCDE assessment, beginning with catastrophic haemorrhage', 3, 'The deck advises a C-ABCDE assessment and identifying the need for further interventions.'],
  ['When should the Major Trauma Tool be considered?', 'When deciding whether the patient is time critical|Only after hospital arrival|Only for minor bruising|Only when the patient has no symptoms', 0, 'The deck directs clinicians to assess whether the patient is time critical and refer to the Major Trauma Tool.'],
  ['What should be looked for when exposing and examining the abdomen?', 'Only skin colour on the hands|Bruising, swelling, lacerations, evisceration and impaled objects|Only the patient temperature|Only the radial pulse', 1, 'The deck lists bruising, swelling, lacerations, evisceration and impaled objects.'],
  ['When may the secondary survey be completed en route to hospital?', 'Only when the patient is completely stable|Only after all dressings are removed|When the patient is time critical and immediate threats have been addressed|Never during transport', 2, 'The deck states that the secondary survey can be completed en route when the patient is time critical, after immediate threats have been addressed.'],
  ['Which approach should be used to assess associated injuries during the secondary survey?', 'Examine only the abdomen|Examine only the lower limbs|Examine only the head and face|Perform a top-to-toe assessment, paying particular attention to the chest, spine and pelvis', 3, 'The deck specifically identifies the chest, spinal and pelvic areas as associated areas to assess.'],
  ['Why is the mechanism of injury important in abdominal trauma?', 'It helps indicate the likelihood and pattern of significant injury|It proves which organ is injured|It replaces the primary survey|It excludes internal bleeding', 0, 'The mechanism guides suspicion and helps identify potentially significant injury, but does not provide a definitive diagnosis.'],
  ['What oxygen guidance does the deck give for abdominal-injury patients?', 'Give oxygen to every patient regardless of findings|Administer oxygen according to JRCALC Plus guidance|Never give oxygen before hospital|Give oxygen only after a scan', 1, 'The deck advises administering oxygen according to JRCALC Plus guidance.'],
  ['According to this deck, which analgesic is within AAP scope for abdominal-injury patients?', 'Any drug requested by the patient|Intravenous morphine without authorisation|Entonox within scope|No analgesia at all', 2, 'The deck states that AAP clinicians can give Entonox for analgesia.'],
  ['What does the deck advise regarding ECG use during abdominal-injury management?', 'Never obtain an ECG|Obtain one only after discharge|Obtain one only for a superficial wound|Monitor vital signs and obtain an ECG if required', 3, 'The management slide states to monitor vital signs and obtain an ECG if required.'],
  ['Why should clinicians avoid trying to name the exact injured abdominal structure prehospitally?', 'The injury may require hospital investigation and cannot be diagnosed by appearance alone|The abdomen contains no organs|All abdominal injuries are minor|The mechanism always gives a definite diagnosis', 0, 'The deck emphasises that clinicians do not have “X-ray eyes” and cannot diagnose the exact injury from the initial appearance.'],
  ['What should be done if an impaled object is pulsating?', 'Remove it immediately|Do not remove or manipulate it, avoid forcibly restricting its pulsation and request senior support|Push it further into the wound|Ignore it during transport', 1, 'The deck states that an in-situ object should be secured, but movement should be allowed if it is pulsating.'],
  ['Why should an evisceration dressing not be wet?', 'Wet dressings always stop bleeding|A wet dressing can become cold and contribute to heat loss|A dry dressing always causes infection|Water replaces the need for assessment', 2, 'The deck warns that a wet dressing can become cold and contribute to heat loss.'],
  ['When should additional clinical support such as APP, HEMS or a paramedic be requested?', 'Only after transport is complete|Only if the patient asks|Only for a minor wound|As soon as the patient requires interventions beyond current scope', 3, 'The deck advises requesting APP, HEMS or paramedic support as soon as other interventions are required.'],
  ['Which action is unsafe in evisceration?', 'Pushing protruding abdominal organs back into the abdominal cavity|Covering the wound with an appropriate dressing|Completing a C-ABCDE assessment|Transporting to appropriate definitive care', 0, 'Protruding organs must not be pushed back into the abdominal cavity.'],
  ['Which summary best reflects the management of abdominal trauma?', 'Focus only on the visible wound and wait for symptoms|Use the mechanism and C-ABCDE assessment, protect exposed organs, escalate early and transport appropriately|Remove penetrating objects before transport|Delay the secondary survey until after hospital arrival in every case', 1, 'The deck emphasises mechanism, C-ABCDE, safe dressing, early escalation, appropriate monitoring and transport.']
]);
addQuestions('poisoning', [
  ['What is a poison?', 'Exposure to a substance that may cause illness or death|Any substance that has a strong smell|A condition caused only by infection|A minor skin irritation', 0, 'The deck defines a poison as exposure to a substance that may result in morbidity or mortality.'],
  ['Which statement about the intent of poisoning is correct?', 'Poisoning is always accidental|It may be intentional, accidental or non-accidental|It is always caused by a prescribed medicine|It can only occur in the workplace', 1, 'The deck states that poisoning may be intentional, accidental or non-accidental.'],
  ['Which is an example of inhalation exposure?', 'Swallowing a tablet|A chemical absorbed through intact skin|Breathing in gases or fumes|A bite from an animal', 2, 'The deck identifies gases and fumes as examples of inhalation exposure.'],
  ['Which is an example of ingestion exposure?', 'A needle injury|A chemical splash to the eye|Breathing smoke|Taking a liquid or solid substance by mouth', 3, 'The deck identifies liquids and solids taken by mouth as ingestion exposure.'],
  ['Which may be an injection route of poisoning?', 'A needle injury or bite|A cosmetic applied to intact skin|A gas in a room|A tablet swallowed normally', 0, 'The deck lists needles and bites as examples of injection exposure.'],
  ['Which may be an absorption route of poisoning?', 'A swallowed solid|A substance entering through the eyes or skin|A needle puncture only|A gas inhaled into the lungs', 1, 'The deck identifies absorption through the eyes or skin.'],
  ['Which option includes types of poison listed in the deck?', 'A fracture or pressure ulcer|A normal meal or clean water|Household products, medicines, recreational substances and chemicals|A healed wound or minor bruise', 2, 'The deck lists household products, pharmaceutical or recreational substances, plants or fungi, alcohol, chemicals and cosmetics.'],
  ['What should be the first clinical approach to a poisoned patient?', 'Identify the exact antidote before assessment|Ask the patient to walk around|Focus only on the container|Complete a primary survey and manage immediate threats', 3, 'The deck prioritises a primary survey before further history, evaluation and treatment.'],
  ['Which information is important when taking a poisoning history?', 'Substance, amount, route, time, intent, symptoms and treatment already given|Only the patient age|Only whether the substance had a smell|Only the patient address', 0, 'A focused history should establish the substance, dose or amount, route, timing, intent, symptoms and treatment.'],
  ['What should be evaluated early in a poisoned patient?', 'Only whether the patient is thirsty|Whether the patient is time critical|Only the colour of the packaging|Whether the patient can walk unaided', 1, 'The deck advises evaluating whether the patient is time critical.'],
  ['Which principle should guide treatment of poisoning?', 'Give the same treatment for every substance|Treat only the visible injury|Base management on identifying the poison, specific treatment and rapid hospital access|Wait for symptoms to disappear before seeking advice', 2, 'The deck identifies poison identification, specific treatment and rapid access to hospital as treatment principles.'],
  ['What should be done with packaging or labels when safe and available?', 'Discard them immediately|Keep relevant information for advice and handover|Hide them from the receiving team|Use them to diagnose the patient without assessment', 1, 'Packaging, labels and medication information may help identify the substance and guide treatment.'],
  ['When should carbon-monoxide poisoning be considered?', 'In a confined space with poor ventilation or a possibly defective boiler|Only after a skin rash develops|Only when the patient has abdominal pain|Only during outdoor exercise', 0, 'The deck highlights confined spaces, impaired ventilation and defective boilers.'],
  ['What is the first priority when carbon-monoxide exposure is possible?', 'Remain in the contaminated area to take a full history|Remove the patient from the source without exposing the crew|Give food and drink before leaving|Open a medicine packet and wait', 1, 'The deck advises removing the patient from the source while maintaining scene safety.'],
  ['Why is scene safety especially important in suspected carbon-monoxide poisoning?', 'The patient may be the only person affected|The source or atmosphere may remain hazardous and affect rescuers or other people|Carbon monoxide is visible in the air|The exposure always stops when the ambulance arrives', 2, 'An unsafe source can expose the crew and other occupants, so do not create additional casualties.'],
  ['What oxygen treatment does the deck advise after carbon-monoxide exposure?', 'No oxygen is required|Low-flow oxygen only for five minutes|Oxygen only if SpO2 is below 90%|After removal from the source, give continuous high-concentration oxygen according to current guidance', 3, 'The deck advises continuous high-concentration oxygen after removing the patient from the source.'],
  ['What is the limitation of standard SpO2 monitoring in suspected carbon-monoxide poisoning?', 'A normal reading cannot reliably detect or exclude carbon-monoxide poisoning|It confirms carbon-monoxide poisoning|It replaces scene assessment|It is the only required observation', 0, 'The deck states that SpO2 monitoring has no value in this situation because it does not reliably identify carbon-monoxide exposure.'],
  ['Which presentation may occur with carbon-monoxide poisoning?', 'Improved concentration and normal consciousness|Disorientation, reduced consciousness or unconsciousness|Only a minor skin irritation|Increased urine output', 1, 'The deck identifies disorientation, reduced consciousness and unconsciousness as possible presentations.'],
  ['What is the appropriate disposition after assessment and immediate treatment of a poisoned patient?', 'Always discharge the patient|Leave the patient at the scene|Transport the patient for further care|Ask the patient to self-medicate', 2, 'The deck advises transport to further care after assessment and appropriate treatment.'],
  ['When should senior or specialist poisoning advice be sought?', 'Only after the patient has recovered|Only if the patient requests it|Only for a minor exposure|When the substance, dose, treatment or scope is uncertain', 3, 'Poison-specific treatment and advice should be obtained when needed, especially outside scope.'],
  ['What should be considered before entering an area where inhaled poison may be present?', 'Scene safety, ventilation and whether the source is still active|Only the patient pain score|Only the patient medication list|Whether the patient has eaten', 0, 'The environment and ongoing source must be considered before crew entry.'],
  ['Why should intent be explored sensitively in poisoning?', 'It determines the colour of the ambulance|Intentional or non-accidental exposure may indicate self-harm or safeguarding concerns|It replaces the clinical assessment|It proves the patient has taken no poison', 1, 'Intent may have self-harm or safeguarding implications and should be assessed sensitively.'],
  ['What may suggest that other people have also been exposed to carbon monoxide?', 'Several occupants in the same environment develop similar symptoms|Only one patient has a minor graze|The boiler is outside in the open air|The patient has a normal appetite', 2, 'Multiple symptomatic people in the same environment should increase concern for a shared exposure.'],
  ['Which history is most relevant after a suspected ingestion?', 'The taste of the substance only|The substance, amount, time, other substances taken and available packaging|The patient sleep pattern only|The patient shoe size only', 3, 'The amount, timing, co-ingestants and substance identity are important.'],
  ['Which route involves a substance entering through the eyes or skin?', 'Absorption|Ingestion|Injection|Inhalation', 0, 'The deck identifies the eyes and skin as absorption routes.'],
  ['What should be done if a patient may still be exposed to an inhaled poison?', 'Remain beside the source without protection|Remove the patient from the source when safe and seek appropriate specialist support|Wait for the source to disappear|Ask an unprotected bystander to enter first', 1, 'Remove the patient from ongoing exposure only when this can be done without exposing rescuers.'],
  ['What should not be done when the poison is unknown?', 'Take a focused history|Keep relevant packaging|Guess an antidote or specific treatment without appropriate advice|Request specialist guidance', 2, 'Treatment should be based on identifying the poison and obtaining appropriate advice.'],
  ['When should a pre-alert be considered for a poisoned patient?', 'Only when the patient is already discharged|Only for a minor exposure|Never for poisoning|For a time-critical patient or hazardous exposure such as carbon monoxide or a chemical incident', 3, 'A pre-alert is appropriate for time-critical patients and hazardous or complex exposures.'],
  ['Which finding may make poisoning time critical?', 'Airway or breathing compromise, reduced consciousness, seizures or ongoing exposure|A normal assessment with no symptoms|A healed skin mark|A known harmless cosmetic product used normally', 0, 'These findings may indicate serious toxicity or ongoing risk and require urgent escalation.'],
  ['Which summary best reflects poisoning management?', 'Identify the poison before doing any primary survey|Prioritise scene safety and the primary survey, identify the route and substance, assess urgency, seek specific advice and transport appropriately|Give oxygen to every poisoned patient regardless of cause|Treat every exposure with the same antidote', 1, 'The deck emphasises scene safety, primary survey, identification, poison-specific treatment, advice and rapid access to hospital.']
]);
addQuestions('urinary-conditions', [
  ['What is acute urinary retention?', 'Inability to empty the bladder that develops suddenly|A minor change in urine colour|A chronic skin condition|An infection of the lungs', 0, 'The deck defines urinary retention as inability to empty the bladder and states that acute retention is a medical emergency.'],
  ['How should acute urinary retention be regarded?', 'As a routine issue that never requires assessment|As a medical emergency|As a normal effect of ageing|As a minor skin condition', 1, 'The deck states that acute urinary retention is a medical emergency.'],
  ['Which may cause acute urinary retention?', 'A healed finger wound|A normal meal|Prostate enlargement, urethral stricture, stones or nerve problems|Improved hydration', 2, 'The deck lists prostate enlargement, urethral stricture, stones and nerve problems among causes.'],
  ['Which presentation is most consistent with acute urinary retention?', 'A mild headache with normal urination|A painless skin rash|Improved urine flow|A painful, distended lower abdomen with inability to pass urine', 3, 'Acute retention may cause severe lower-abdominal pain, inability to pass urine, reduced flow and bladder distension.'],
  ['What complication can occur if urinary retention is not treated promptly?', 'Urine may pass back towards the kidneys and damage them|Vision improves|The lungs become stronger|Blood sugar always falls', 0, 'The deck warns that urine may pass back up into the kidneys and damage them.'],
  ['What usually causes a urinary-tract infection?', 'A fracture|Bacteria infecting part of the urinary tract|A lack of oxygen in the lungs|A skin bruise', 1, 'The deck states that a UTI usually develops when bacteria infect part of the urinary tract.'],
  ['Why may a UTI in a man require investigation for an underlying cause?', 'UTIs cannot occur in men|Men never have urinary symptoms|UTIs are less common in men, so an underlying cause should be sought|UTIs in men are always caused by dehydration', 2, 'The deck states that UTIs are less common in men and may require investigation for an underlying cause.'],
  ['Why can an upper UTI be more serious than a lower UTI?', 'It causes only mild bladder discomfort|It never causes fever|It affects only the urethra|It can involve the kidneys and risk kidney damage', 3, 'Upper UTIs affect the kidneys or ureters and carry a risk of kidney damage.'],
  ['Which is a possible symptom of a UTI?', 'Pain or burning when urinating with increased frequency|Improved urine flow|Only a painless ankle injury|A normal appetite with no urinary symptoms', 0, 'UTI symptoms include dysuria, burning and increased frequency.'],
  ['Which combination may suggest kidney infection in a patient with urinary symptoms?', 'A normal temperature and no pain|Temperature above 38 degrees Celsius, rigors and vomiting|A healed wound|Improved exercise tolerance', 1, 'The deck lists temperature above 38, rigors and vomiting as possible signs of kidney infection.'],
  ['What are kidney stones?', 'Infections of the lungs|Blood clots in the heart|Stone-like lumps that can develop in one or both kidneys|A type of skin ulcer', 2, 'The deck describes kidney stones as stone-like lumps that may form in one or both kidneys.'],
  ['What is renal colic?', 'A painless skin change|A chronic cough|A normal urination pattern|Intense intermittent pain in the back or side of the abdomen, often with restlessness', 3, 'Renal colic is described as periods of intense pain lasting minutes or hours, with restlessness often present.'],
  ['Which combination may occur with a kidney stone?', 'Blood in the urine, nausea or vomiting and pain towards the groin|Improved hearing with no urinary symptoms|A painless rash with normal renal function|Normal urine output with no pain or urinary symptoms', 0, 'Kidney stones may cause haematuria, nausea or vomiting, pain and urinary symptoms.'],
  ['What is acute kidney injury?', 'A condition that develops only over many years|A sudden episode of kidney damage or failure occurring within hours or days|A superficial bladder infection only|A normal response to exercise', 1, 'The deck defines AKI as sudden kidney damage or failure occurring within hours or days.'],
  ['Why does acute kidney injury require early treatment?', 'It always resolves without treatment|It only affects the skin|Waste products, electrolytes and other chemicals can build up and affect other organs|It cannot progress to kidney failure', 2, 'The deck emphasises early detection and treatment because waste products and abnormal chemicals can affect other organs.'],
  ['Which finding may occur in acute kidney injury?', 'Improved urine output only|Normal hydration in every case|A healed wound|Less than 100 ml of urine in 24 hours, confusion, drowsiness, dehydration or vomiting', 3, 'The deck lists reduced urine output, including less than 100 ml in 24 hours, confusion, drowsiness, dehydration and nausea or vomiting.'],
  ['What commonly causes AKI according to the deck?', 'Reduced blood flow to the kidneys|Improved cardiac output|A minor skin mark|Increased hearing', 0, 'Most cases of AKI in the deck are described as being caused by reduced renal blood flow.'],
  ['Which may reduce blood flow to the kidneys and contribute to AKI?', 'A normal appetite|Low blood volume, reduced cardiac output, vessel problems or certain medicines|A healed bruise|A normal urine stream', 1, 'The deck lists low blood volume, reduced cardiac output, blood-vessel problems and certain medicines.'],
  ['Which is an obstructive cause of acute kidney injury?', 'Improved hydration|A normal blood pressure|An enlarged prostate, pelvic tumour or kidney stone|A minor headache', 2, 'The deck identifies enlarged prostate, pelvic tumours and kidney stones as possible obstructive causes.'],
  ['Which may be a serious complication of AKI?', 'Improved lung function|A normal potassium level|A minor skin change|High potassium, pulmonary oedema or metabolic acidosis', 3, 'The deck lists hyperkalaemia, pulmonary oedema and metabolic acidosis as serious complications.'],
  ['What is chronic kidney disease?', 'A long-term condition in which the kidneys do not function as well as they should|A sudden ankle injury|A short-lived bladder spasm|An infection that always resolves within a day', 0, 'The deck describes CKD as a long-term condition where kidney function is reduced.'],
  ['Which symptom may occur in more advanced chronic kidney disease?', 'Improved energy and no swelling|Tiredness, swollen ankles, shortness of breath or nausea|Only a healed cut|Only increased hearing', 1, 'The deck lists tiredness, swelling, shortness of breath, nausea and other symptoms in advanced CKD.'],
  ['Which condition is associated with chronic kidney disease?', 'A healed finger wound|A normal blood pressure in every case|Diabetes, hypertension, kidney infection or urinary obstruction|Improved exercise tolerance', 2, 'The deck identifies diabetes, high blood pressure, kidney infections and urinary blockages among associated causes.'],
  ['Which may be part of chronic kidney disease treatment?', 'Only a plaster|Only a short course of rest|No follow-up is required|Lifestyle changes, medication, dialysis or kidney transplant', 3, 'The deck lists lifestyle change, medicines, dialysis and kidney transplant depending on cause and severity.'],
  ['What general prehospital approach does the deck recommend for urinary conditions?', 'Give oxygen only according to JRCALC and clinical need, provide pain relief as required, monitor observations and transfer for further care|Ignore vital signs if pain is present|Delay transport until the patient can urinate normally|Treat only the urine colour', 0, 'The management slide advises oxygen according to JRCALC and clinical need, analgesia, constant observations and transfer to further care.'],
  ['When does the deck suggest considering a 12-lead ECG in a urinary patient?', 'Only for a minor skin complaint|In an older patient or one with cardiac risk factors and upper-abdominal pain|Only after discharge|Never in urinary conditions', 1, 'The deck highlights a 12-lead ECG for older patients and those with cardiac risk factors and upper-abdominal pain.'],
  ['What should be considered when a urinary patient is systemically unwell?', 'Only a minor bladder irritation|Only a chronic skin condition|Sepsis and treatment according to relevant guidelines|No further assessment', 2, 'The management slide specifically advises considering sepsis and treating according to guidelines.'],
  ['When may a urinary catheter be used in AKI management?', 'For every patient with a UTI|Only to measure blood pressure|Never when there is obstruction|To drain the bladder when a blockage is causing retention', 3, 'The deck lists catheterisation to drain the bladder when there is a blockage.'],
  ['What may happen to a small kidney stone?', 'It may pass painlessly in the urine and go undetected|It always causes complete kidney failure|It always requires surgery|It can never move into a ureter', 0, 'The deck states that small stones may pass painlessly and go unnoticed.'],
  ['Which summary best reflects assessment of urinary conditions?', 'Assess only the patient pain score and provide reassurance|Assess urinary symptoms, pain, hydration, observations and sepsis risk, then follow scope and transfer appropriately|Assume all urinary symptoms are minor and need no monitoring|Avoid ECG and vital-sign monitoring unless the patient requests it', 1, 'The deck supports a structured assessment, monitoring, sepsis consideration, analgesia and transfer for further care.']
]);
addQuestions('addisons-disease', [
  ['What is Addison\'s disease?', 'Primary adrenal insufficiency caused by damage to the adrenal cortex|A condition affecting only the lungs|A form of diabetes|A minor skin disorder', 0, 'The deck identifies Addison\'s disease as primary adrenal insufficiency caused by adrenal-cortex damage.'],
  ['What can cause secondary adrenal insufficiency?', 'A minor ankle injury|A dysfunctional pituitary gland or prolonged steroid exposure|A high-carbohydrate meal|A superficial skin infection only', 1, 'The deck identifies pituitary dysfunction and long-term or high-dose steroid exposure as causes.'],
  ['Which two hormones are emphasised in the deck as essential adrenal hormones?', 'Insulin and glucagon|Adrenaline and dopamine|Cortisol and aldosterone|Thyroxine and melatonin', 2, 'The deck identifies cortisol and aldosterone as essential adrenal hormones.'],
  ['Which combination describes roles of cortisol and aldosterone?', 'They only control eyesight|They only regulate body temperature|They have no role during physiological stress|They help regulate blood pressure, immune function, stress response and blood salts', 3, 'The deck lists blood-pressure regulation, immune function, stress response and salt regulation among their roles.'],
  ['What happens in primary adrenal insufficiency?', 'The adrenal cortex cannot produce enough cortisol and aldosterone|The pituitary produces too much insulin|The kidneys stop filtering blood immediately|The lungs cannot exchange oxygen', 0, 'Damage to the adrenal cortex reduces production of cortisol and aldosterone.'],
  ['How may the pituitary contribute to secondary adrenal insufficiency?', 'It produces too much aldosterone|It fails to signal the adrenal glands to produce cortisol|It causes a direct fracture of the adrenal gland|It increases blood glucose in every patient', 1, 'A dysfunctional pituitary gland may fail to signal the adrenal glands to make cortisol.'],
  ['Which skin findings may be associated with Addison\'s disease?', 'A fresh laceration|A pressure ulcer|Hyperpigmentation or vitiligo|A normal skin tone', 2, 'The deck illustrates hyperpigmentation and vitiligo as possible associated findings, but they are not diagnostic on their own.'],
  ['Which is a possible trigger of an adrenal crisis?', 'A routine conversation|A healed bruise|Normal hydration|Infection, trauma, surgery, burns or sudden steroid cessation', 3, 'The deck lists infection, injury, surgery, burns and sudden stopping of steroids as possible triggers.'],
  ['How can prolonged steroid treatment affect adrenal function?', 'It can suppress the body\'s own cortisol production|It always increases natural cortisol production|It prevents all infections|It permanently improves adrenal function', 0, 'The deck warns that prolonged or high-dose steroid treatment can suppress endogenous cortisol production.'],
  ['How long may the effect of steroid-related adrenal suppression last after stopping treatment according to the supplied deck?', 'One day only|Up to a year|Only until the next meal|It always ends immediately', 1, 'The supplied deck states that the effect may last as long as a year after steroid discontinuation. Follow current clinical guidance in practice.'],
  ['Why can an adrenal crisis develop during illness or injury?', 'The body produces too much cortisol|The adrenal glands stop responding to oxygen|The body cannot increase cortisol production enough to meet stress demands|The patient has too many red blood cells', 2, 'During stress, the body may be unable to increase cortisol production sufficiently.'],
  ['Which presentation should raise concern for adrenal crisis?', 'A minor bruise with no symptoms|Improved appetite and normal activity|A healed scratch|Collapse or hypotension with hypoglycaemia, vomiting, diarrhoea or severe weakness', 3, 'Adrenal crisis may cause rapid deterioration with circulatory, glucose and gastrointestinal symptoms.'],
  ['What is the initial assessment approach for suspected adrenal crisis?', 'Assess and manage the ABCs symptomatically|Wait for laboratory confirmation before assessing the patient|Focus only on the skin|Ask the patient to walk around', 0, 'The deck advises assessing and managing ABC symptomatically.'],
  ['What oxygen target is stated in the supplied deck, subject to current JRCALC and local guidance?', 'SpO2 below 80%|SpO2 above 94%|SpO2 exactly 100% in every patient|No oxygen monitoring is required', 1, 'The supplied deck advises maintaining SpO2 above 94%; current JRCALC and local guidance should take precedence.'],
  ['What is the scope position for 100 mg hydrocortisone IV or IM in the deck?', 'It is routine AAP treatment|It should be given by anyone at scene|It is not within AAP scope of practice|It is never used in adrenal crisis', 2, 'The deck lists 100 mg hydrocortisone IV or IM but states it is not within AAP scope.'],
  ['Who may manage hypoglycaemia with IV glucose according to the deck?', 'Any bystander|Only the patient|Only a family member|A paramedic, where required and authorised', 3, 'The deck identifies IV glucose for hypoglycaemia as a paramedic intervention where required.'],
  ['When should IV access be considered in suspected adrenal crisis according to the deck?', 'When indicated as part of senior or paramedic management|Only after discharge|Never during an emergency|Only for a minor skin complaint', 0, 'The deck identifies IV access if indicated as a paramedic intervention.'],
  ['What cardiac assessment is included in the management slide?', 'No cardiac assessment|An ECG|Only a temperature check|Only a visual skin assessment', 1, 'The deck includes ECG as part of adrenal-crisis management.'],
  ['Where should a patient with suspected adrenal crisis be transported?', 'To a routine outpatient appointment|To a minor-injury clinic only|Rapidly to the nearest suitable emergency department|Home for observation in every case', 2, 'The deck advises rapid transport to the nearest suitable emergency department.'],
  ['Which statement best describes cortisol during physiological stress?', 'It has no role in stress response|It only regulates vision|It is produced only after eating|It helps the body respond to stress and supports blood-pressure regulation', 3, 'Cortisol assists the stress response and contributes to blood-pressure regulation.'],
  ['Which role is associated with aldosterone?', 'Helping regulate salts in the blood and blood pressure|Producing red blood cells directly|Controlling hearing|Digesting proteins in the stomach', 0, 'The deck states that aldosterone helps regulate salts in the blood and blood pressure.'],
  ['Which history is most relevant when assessing possible adrenal crisis?', 'The patient shoe size|Known adrenal insufficiency, steroid medication and recent missed or stopped doses|Only the patient favourite meal|A healed childhood scar', 1, 'Ask about the diagnosis, steroid treatment, recent medication changes and the patient\'s emergency plan.'],
  ['Which condition is listed as a possible trigger for adrenal crisis?', 'A minor paper cut only|A normal day without stress|A major infection or myocardial infarction|Improved sleep', 2, 'The deck lists major infection and medical emergencies such as myocardial infarction as possible triggers.'],
  ['Why can suddenly stopping steroid treatment be dangerous?', 'It always improves adrenal function|It prevents the need for cortisol|It has no physiological effect|It may leave the body unable to increase cortisol production during stress', 3, 'Sudden steroid cessation can increase the risk of adrenal crisis because cortisol production may remain suppressed.'],
  ['Which metabolic problem should be assessed in an unwell patient with possible adrenal crisis?', 'Hypoglycaemia|Improved glucose tolerance|High vision pressure|A healed fracture', 0, 'Hypoglycaemia is listed as a possible trigger and a clinical problem requiring management.'],
  ['Why should vital signs be monitored repeatedly?', 'A single normal reading rules out adrenal crisis|The patient can deteriorate rapidly and trends may show circulatory compromise|Vital signs are unrelated to adrenal function|Only the patient temperature can change', 1, 'Rapid deterioration is possible, so trends in observations help identify worsening shock or other compromise.'],
  ['Which intervention is outside AAP scope according to the deck?', 'Taking a history|Measuring oxygen saturation|Administering 100 mg hydrocortisone IV or IM|Recording a pain score', 2, 'The deck specifically states that hydrocortisone 100 mg IV or IM is not within AAP scope.'],
  ['Which condition may resemble adrenal crisis and should remain in the differential diagnosis?', 'A normal appetite|A healed scar|A minor change in clothing|Sepsis, hypoglycaemia, shock or another medical emergency', 3, 'The presentation can overlap with sepsis, hypoglycaemia, shock and other emergencies.'],
  ['What medication information should be included in the assessment?', 'Steroid name, dose, last dose, missed doses and any emergency plan|Only the colour of the tablets|Only the patient preferred pharmacy|No medication information is needed', 0, 'Medication and emergency-plan details help identify adrenal suppression and guide escalation.'],
  ['Which summary best reflects management of suspected adrenal crisis?', 'Wait for a definitive diagnosis before acting|Assess ABCs, check glucose and vital signs, follow scope and current guidance, request senior support and transport rapidly|Give hydrocortisone regardless of scope|Treat only the patient\'s skin findings', 1, 'The deck emphasises symptomatic ABC management, glucose and ECG assessment, scope-aware interventions, senior support and rapid ED transport.']
]);
addQuestions('head-injuries', [
  ['Which is a common cause of traumatic brain injury?', 'A road traffic collision, fall, assault or direct blow to the head|A minor skin infection|A routine meal|A pressure ulcer', 0, 'The deck identifies road traffic collisions, falls, assaults and direct blows as common causes.'],
  ['Which mechanism is associated with particularly poor outcomes in head injury?', 'A minor superficial graze|A penetrating injury or ejection from a vehicle|A healed bruise|A low-energy movement with no impact', 1, 'The deck highlights penetrating injuries and ejection from vehicles among mechanisms associated with the worst outcomes.'],
  ['What is a primary brain injury?', 'An injury that develops only after hospital admission|A complication caused only by hypoxia|An instantaneous injury resulting from the impact itself|A condition caused by low blood pressure after treatment', 2, 'A primary brain injury occurs at the moment of impact.'],
  ['How can prehospital care influence secondary brain injury?', 'By reversing all primary brain damage|By delaying transport for a detailed examination|By diagnosing the exact brain structure injured|By recognising TBI, managing the patient appropriately and preventing avoidable deterioration', 3, 'Good prehospital management can help reduce secondary injury by recognising and managing TBI appropriately.'],
  ['What is traumatic brain injury?', 'Brain injury caused by trauma to the head|Any injury to the scalp only|A fracture of the arm|A condition caused only by infection', 0, 'The deck defines TBI as brain injury caused by trauma to the head.'],
  ['Which is a complication of traumatic brain injury?', 'Improved oxygenation|Hypoxaemia, hypotension or intracerebral haematoma|A healed scalp wound|Increased appetite', 1, 'The deck identifies hypoxaemia, hypotension and intracerebral haematoma as complications.'],
  ['Why can scalp injuries appear worse than they are?', 'The scalp has no blood vessels|The scalp cannot swell|The scalp is highly vascular and may bleed significantly|Scalp injuries always involve the brain', 2, 'The scalp is highly vascular, so injuries may look dramatic and can cause significant bleeding.'],
  ['What does a skull fracture indicate?', 'A minor injury in every case|Only an injury to the scalp|That no brain injury is possible|Significant force and possible damage to underlying brain structures', 3, 'A skull fracture indicates significant force and may damage structures beneath it.'],
  ['What are "panda eyes" in the context of head injury?', 'Periorbital bruising|A fixed dilated pupil|A scalp laceration|Bruising over the shin', 0, 'Panda eyes refers to periorbital ecchymosis.'],
  ['What should be done if blood or cerebrospinal fluid is leaking from the nose or ears?', 'Pack the nose or ear tightly|Do not attempt to stop the flow and arrange prompt assessment|Insert an object to block the leak|Apply direct pressure to an open skull fracture', 1, 'The deck advises not attempting to stop blood or CSF flow from the nose or ears.'],
  ['Which symptom may suggest traumatic brain injury?', 'Improved concentration only|A healed hand wound|Amnesia, severe headache, vomiting or altered consciousness|A normal appetite', 2, 'The deck lists amnesia, severe headache, nausea or vomiting and altered consciousness among suggestive symptoms.'],
  ['What can a sluggish pupil indicate?', 'Only dehydration|A healed eye injury|Normal neurological function in every case|Early raised intracranial pressure or cerebral hypoxia', 3, 'A sluggish pupil may be an early sign of raised ICP or cerebral hypoxia.'],
  ['What does the deck identify as the best indicator of a head-injured patient condition?', 'Level of consciousness|Scalp colour alone|Blood pressure alone|The patient age', 0, 'The deck identifies level of consciousness as the best indicator of condition.'],
  ['What is the relationship between AVPU and GCS?', 'AVPU always replaces GCS|AVPU provides a baseline but is not a substitute for a full GCS|GCS is only used for limb injuries|Neither is useful in head injury', 1, 'The deck states that AVPU is a baseline assessment and not a substitute for calculating the full GCS.'],
  ['Which three measurements form the Glasgow Coma Scale?', 'Blood pressure, pulse and temperature|Pain, pupils and capillary refill|Eye opening, verbal response and motor response|Airway, breathing and circulation', 2, 'GCS is based on eye opening, verbal response and motor response.'],
  ['What is the range of possible GCS scores?', '1 to 5|5 to 20|10 to 30|3 to 15', 3, 'The GCS score ranges from 3 to 15.'],
  ['Which GCS range is identified as severe traumatic brain injury in the supplied deck?', '3-8 (the source band overlaps at 8)|9-12|13-15|16-20', 0, 'The deck identifies GCS 3-8 as severe and 8-12 as moderate, creating an overlap at 8. Use current local guidance when applying a boundary score.'],
  ['Why should cervical-spine injury be considered in a significant head injury?', 'Reduced consciousness has a significant association with cervical-spine injury|Head injury always excludes spinal injury|The cervical spine cannot be injured in a fall|Only a limb injury can affect the spine', 1, 'The deck states that reduced consciousness is significantly associated with cervical-spine injury.'],
  ['What is a cerebral contusion?', 'A fracture of the facial bones|A blood clot in a leg|Bruising and swelling caused when the brain hits the inside of the skull|A superficial scalp infection', 2, 'A cerebral contusion is bruising and swelling from the brain striking the inside of the skull.'],
  ['Which types of intracranial haemorrhage are identified in the deck?', 'Only external scalp bleeding|Only nasal bleeding|Only bleeding from the ears|Extradural, subdural and subarachnoid haemorrhage', 3, 'The deck identifies extradural, subdural and subarachnoid haemorrhage.'],
  ['Why may higher mean arterial pressure be needed after traumatic brain injury?', 'Blood flow in affected areas may change and higher MAP may be needed to maintain perfusion|The brain becomes immune to hypotension|Oxygen is no longer needed|Intracranial pressure always falls', 0, 'The deck explains that affected brain areas may rely on higher-than-normal mean arterial pressure to maintain perfusion.'],
  ['Why is early detection of raised intracranial pressure critical?', 'It always resolves without treatment|Increasing pressure can stop cerebral blood flow and force the brain downward|It only affects the scalp|It prevents all primary brain injury', 1, 'The deck warns that increasing ICP can stop cerebral blood flow and force the brain downward into the spinal canal.'],
  ['Which combination is associated with Cushing’s triad described in the deck?', 'Low blood pressure, fast pulse and regular breathing|Low temperature, slow breathing and equal pupils|Raised blood pressure, falling heart rate and irregular respirations|A normal pulse and improved consciousness', 2, 'The deck describes raised blood pressure, falling heart rate and irregular respirations in response to rising intracranial pressure.'],
  ['Which set of aims describes prehospital management of head injury?', 'Prevent hypoxia, treat hypotension, maintain appropriate ETCO2 and transport promptly|Delay transport until every symptom resolves|Treat only visible scalp wounds|Avoid monitoring neurological status', 0, 'The prehospital aims are to prevent hypoxia, treat hypotension, maintain appropriate ETCO2 and transport promptly.'],
  ['Which support may a paramedic provide for a serious head injury?', 'Only a wound dressing|Assistance with advanced airway management procedures|Only a blood-pressure reading|Only a pain score', 1, 'The deck states that a paramedic can assist with advanced airway management procedures.'],
  ['What should not be done to an open or depressed skull fracture?', 'Protect the patient from further injury|Request senior support|Apply pressure directly to the fracture|Monitor consciousness and pupils', 2, 'The deck specifically states not to apply pressure to open or depressed skull fractures.'],
  ['What should be avoided when managing a head-injured patient with blood or CSF from the nose or ears?', 'Repeated neurological assessment|Appropriate airway management|Prompt transport|Trying to stop the flow from the nose or ears', 3, 'The deck advises not attempting to stop blood or CSF flow from the nose or ears.'],
  ['What does hypotension or tachycardia indicate in a patient described as having an isolated head injury?', 'Other injuries are present and should be sought|The head injury is definitely mild|The patient has no significant injury|The patient only needs a dressing', 0, 'The deck states that isolated head injury does not cause hypotension or tachycardia, so signs of shock indicate other injuries are present.'],
  ['What does the deck advise for a head-injured patient taking warfarin?', 'Discharge if the patient appears well|Convey to an emergency department for CT within 8 hours under the stated pathway, regardless of apparent severity|Avoid all assessment|Wait for symptoms to develop at home', 1, 'The deck states that head-injured patients taking warfarin should be conveyed for CT within 8 hours, with the stated radiology-report pathway.'],
  ['Which summary best reflects safe head-injury management?', 'Focus only on the scalp wound|Repeatedly assess AVPU, GCS and pupils, prevent hypoxia, treat hypotension, protect the C-spine, request senior input early and transport promptly|Delay transport for a complete history|Apply pressure to every skull fracture', 1, 'The deck summary emphasises neurological reassessment, prevention of hypoxia, treatment of hypotension, C-spine protection, early senior support and prompt transport.']
]);
addQuestions('sepsis', [
  ['What is sepsis?', 'Life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated response to infection|A minor local skin infection|A normal immune response without organ effects|A condition caused only by blood loss', 0, 'The deck defines sepsis as life-threatening organ dysfunction due to a dysregulated host response to infection.'],
  ['How does the deck describe suspected sepsis?', 'A confirmed specific illness|A clinical syndrome rather than a specific illness|A diagnosis made by NEWS2 alone|A condition that only affects the lungs', 1, 'The deck states that suspected sepsis is a syndrome and not a specific illness.'],
  ['Which combination characterises septic shock in the supplied deck?', 'Normal blood pressure and no organ effects|A local infection with no systemic features|Low blood pressure despite adequate fluid replacement with organ dysfunction or failure|A raised temperature alone', 2, 'The deck describes sepsis with shock, or septic shock, as low blood pressure despite adequate fluid replacement together with organ dysfunction or failure.'],
  ['What can happen to blood vessels during sepsis?', 'They always become stronger|They stop carrying blood permanently|They become completely rigid|They may become leaky, allowing fluid to shift into tissues', 3, 'Inflammation can damage the vessel lining and cause fluid to shift into tissues.'],
  ['Why can organ failure develop in severe sepsis?', 'Low blood pressure and leaky vessels reduce oxygen and nutrient delivery to organs|Organs receive too much oxygen|The infection only affects the skin|The heart always stops immediately', 0, 'Reduced perfusion and oxygen delivery can cause organs to fail.'],
  ['What is the first stage of DIC described in the deck?', 'The body stops producing all white blood cells|Overactive clotting blocks blood flow and can damage organs|The patient develops a simple bruise only|All bleeding immediately stops permanently', 1, 'DIC can begin with overactive clotting that blocks blood flow and causes organ damage.'],
  ['Which finding may occur in DIC associated with sepsis?', 'Improved exercise tolerance|A healed scar|Petechiae or gastrointestinal bleeding|A normal appetite alone', 2, 'The deck lists petechiae and gastrointestinal bleeding among findings associated with abnormal clotting.'],
  ['Which may be a source of sepsis?', 'Only a fracture|Only a headache|Only a minor bruise|Pneumonia, urinary infection, cellulitis or an infected wound', 3, 'The deck lists chest, urinary, skin, wound, neurological, abdominal and device-related sources.'],
  ['Which patient is identified as a higher-risk group for sepsis?', 'A person aged over 75|A healthy adult with no risk factors|A person with a healed graze only|A person with no medical history', 0, 'People over 75 are listed as a higher-risk group.'],
  ['What does DAMN represent in the sepsis risk-group section?', 'Diabetes, asthma, meningitis and nausea|Diuretics, ACE inhibitors, metformin and NSAIDs|Disability, airway, movement and nutrition|Dizziness, anaemia, migraine and nephritis', 1, 'The deck uses DAMN for diuretics, ACE inhibitors, metformin and NSAIDs.'],
  ['Which statement about pregnancy and NEWS2 is given in the supplied deck and should be checked against current local guidance?', 'NEWS2 is required for every pregnant patient|Pregnancy has no relevance to sepsis risk|NEWS2 is not to be used with pregnant patients|NEWS2 replaces clinical assessment during pregnancy', 2, 'The deck identifies recent pregnancy as a risk and states that NEWS2 is not to be used with pregnant patients. Apply current local pregnancy-specific guidance.'],
  ['Which infant is identified as a higher-risk group?', 'A child aged 10 with no symptoms|A healthy adult|A teenager with a minor graze|A baby younger than one year', 3, 'Babies younger than one year are identified as a risk group.'],
  ['What is the role of neutrophils?', 'They help fight infection as a type of white blood cell|They regulate bone movement|They carry oxygen like red blood cells|They produce digestive enzymes only', 0, 'Neutrophils are white blood cells that play a key role in fighting infection.'],
  ['How does the deck define neutropaenic sepsis?', 'Any cough in a healthy adult|A temperature above 38 degrees Celsius, or any symptoms or signs of sepsis, in a patient with a lowered neutrophil count|Only a low blood pressure reading|A local wound with no systemic symptoms', 1, 'The deck gives a temperature above 38 degrees Celsius or symptoms or signs of sepsis with a lowered neutrophil count.'],
  ['Why can neutropaenic sepsis progress rapidly?', 'The immune system is stronger than usual|There is never any organ damage|The body has reduced ability to fight infection|It only affects the skin surface', 2, 'Reduced neutrophils weaken the immune response, allowing the sepsis process to progress faster.'],
  ['Which age group should use the adult sepsis screening tool in the deck?', 'People aged five and under|People aged 12 and under|People aged 18 and over only|People aged 16 years and over', 3, 'The deck identifies the adult tool for people aged 16 years and over.'],
  ['What is the relationship between NEWS2 and sepsis diagnosis?', 'NEWS2 does not diagnose sepsis and must be interpreted with the wider clinical assessment|NEWS2 alone confirms sepsis|NEWS2 is not used with any adult assessment|A normal NEWS2 always excludes sepsis', 0, 'The deck explicitly states that NEWS2 does not diagnose sepsis.'],
  ['During the primary survey, which neurological change should raise concern?', 'Improved concentration after rest|Confusion or a reduced level of consciousness|A normal conversation|A healed wound', 1, 'The primary survey includes checking for confusion or reduced level of consciousness.'],
  ['Which combination belongs in a secondary-survey history for suspected sepsis?', 'The patient shoe size and favourite meal|Only the time of the last meal|SAMPLE, infection history and relevant past medical history|Only recent travel history', 2, 'The deck advises SAMPLE, infection history, duration, past medical history and other focused questions.'],
  ['Which examination is listed in the secondary-survey section?', 'Only a knee examination|Only a hearing test|Only a skin-temperature check|Chest auscultation, checking for rashes and a review of systems', 3, 'The deck lists chest auscultation, rashes and review of systems.'],
  ['Which observation should be monitored for confusion and neurological deterioration?', 'Level of consciousness|Hair colour|Hand dominance|Visual acuity only', 0, 'The deck specifically highlights monitoring level of consciousness and confusion.'],
  ['Why should blood pressure not be used alone to exclude sepsis?', 'Blood pressure always rises in sepsis|The deck identifies blood pressure as an unreliable indicator of sepsis|Blood pressure cannot be measured in adults|Blood pressure is unrelated to circulation', 1, 'The deck notes that blood pressure can be an unreliable indicator of sepsis.'],
  ['Which presentation should prompt consideration of suspected sepsis according to the deck?', 'Only a visible wound|Only a patient with no infection history|Fever or an unwell patient with NEWS2 of 5 or more, and/or an unwell appearance with a history of infection|Only a confirmed infection after hospital scanning', 2, 'The deck advises considering sepsis with fever or an unwell patient and NEWS2 of at least 5 and/or an unwell appearance with a history of infection.'],
  ['What should happen to on-scene time when sepsis is suspected?', 'It should be extended for non-essential tasks|The patient should walk around before transport|No observations should be taken|Keep on-scene time to a minimum', 3, 'The deck specifically advises keeping on-scene times to a minimum.'],
  ['What should a pre-alert include when sepsis is suspected?', 'The NEWS2 score and the clinical concern for sepsis|Only the patient name|Only the location of the ambulance station|Only the patient shoe size', 0, 'The deck advises providing a pre-alert and NEWS2 score when sepsis is suspected.'],
  ['Which intervention may require senior-clinician involvement according to the deck?', 'Offering the patient water|Giving benzylpenicillin, IV PAR or sodium chloride|Taking a basic history|Recording the patient temperature', 1, 'The deck identifies senior-clinician involvement for treatments such as benzylpenicillin, IV PAR and sodium chloride.'],
  ['What should be done if the infection source is not obvious?', 'Exclude sepsis immediately|Assume the patient is well|Continue assessment because infection may be suspected without an obvious source|Wait until symptoms have lasted one month', 2, 'The deck states that infection can be suspected even when the source is not obvious.'],
  ['Which patient is included in the pregnancy-related sepsis risk group?', 'Only a patient who has never been pregnant|Only a patient more than ten years after pregnancy|Only a patient with a minor ankle injury|A woman who has recently given birth or recently been pregnant, including after miscarriage or abortion', 3, 'The deck includes recent birth, pregnancy, miscarriage and abortion in this risk group.'],
  ['How should temperature be treated within the sepsis assessment?', 'It has limited value on its own and must be interpreted with the wider assessment|It confirms sepsis by itself|It should never be recorded|It replaces respiratory and circulatory observations', 0, 'The deck notes that temperature has limited value and should not be used alone.'],
  ['Which summary best reflects the approach to suspected sepsis?', 'Wait for NEWS2 to confirm sepsis before escalating|Use clinical assessment, select the adult or paediatric screening tool by age, follow pregnancy-specific guidance, minimise delay, pre-alert and escalate early|Focus only on temperature|Treat every patient with antibiotics regardless of scope', 1, 'The deck emphasises clinical assessment, the correct age-specific screening tool, pregnancy considerations, short on-scene times, pre-alerting and early escalation.']
]);
addQuestions('wounds-and-bleeding', [
  ['What is a wound?', 'A break in the continuity of tissue|A normal area of intact skin|A type of infection only|A change in blood pressure', 0, 'The deck defines a wound as a break in the continuity of tissue.'],
  ['What is a closed wound?', 'A wound that always causes external bleeding|Damage to soft tissues beneath intact skin|A wound caused only by a sharp object|A wound that cannot involve a fracture', 1, 'A closed wound damages soft tissues beneath the surface without breaking the skin.'],
  ['Why can an open wound be clinically significant?', 'It never causes blood loss|It only affects the outer skin|It exposes underlying tissue to infection and may involve deeper damage|It cannot affect organs or bones', 2, 'Open wounds expose tissue to infection and may involve blood loss, bone, vessels or organs.'],
  ['Which wound is usually caused by blunt trauma and damaged blood vessels beneath the skin?', 'An incision|A puncture wound|A laceration|A contusion', 3, 'A contusion is usually caused by blunt trauma and damaged blood vessels under the skin.'],
  ['What usually causes a laceration?', 'Snagging or tearing of tissue|A clean sharp cut only|A pointed object entering the body|A projectile pressure wave only', 0, 'A laceration usually results from snagging or tearing of tissue.'],
  ['Which description best fits an incision?', 'A wound caused by blunt trauma|A usually clean wound made by a sharp-edged object|A wound that never bleeds|A closed wound beneath intact skin', 1, 'An incision is usually made by a sharp-edged object and is often clean.'],
  ['What should be assumed when assessing a puncture or penetrating wound?', 'The wound is always superficial|The object has caused no internal damage|Underlying structures may be damaged|There is no risk if external bleeding is small', 2, 'Penetrating wounds can hide internal damage. Do not probe or remove impaled objects.'],
  ['What should be done with an impaled object?', 'Pull it out quickly|Turn it to find the direction of injury|Push it further in to control bleeding|Leave it in situ and stabilise it without removing or probing it', 3, 'Removing or probing an impaled object may worsen bleeding or damage underlying structures.'],
  ['What is the priority when managing a gunshot or blast injury at scene?', 'Crew safety|Identifying the type of gun|Finding the projectile before assessment|Treating only the visible wound', 0, 'The deck states that crew safety is paramount in gunshot and blast injuries.'],
  ['What is a temporary cavity in a cavitation injury?', 'A skin infection around the wound|A space formed when a projectile pressure wave pushes tissue away|A normal anatomical space|A cavity caused only by blunt bruising', 1, 'A high-energy projectile can create a temporary cavity by pushing surrounding tissue away.'],
  ['What is an amputation?', 'A superficial bruise|A type of pressure ulcer|Loss of a body part caused by an accident or injury|A clean incision that does not affect tissue', 2, 'The deck defines amputation as the loss of a body part caused by accident or injury.'],
  ['What is a degloving injury?', 'A puncture caused by a pointed object|A collection of blood under the skin|A clean cut from a sharp edge|Skin and soft tissue torn away by shearing forces', 3, 'Degloving occurs when layers of skin and soft tissue are torn away through shearing forces.'],
  ['How may a small foreign object in a wound be managed when appropriate?', 'Wash it from the wound with water|Probe around it with an instrument|Push it deeper into the wound|Cover it without assessing it', 0, 'The deck states that small foreign objects may be washed from a wound with water.'],
  ['What is the correct approach to a large foreign object that is not easily removed?', 'Remove it with force|Leave it in situ and secure it with dressings|Probe around it to identify its path|Cut it shorter before transport', 1, 'Larger objects should be left in situ and secured to prevent movement.'],
  ['Which description is associated with arterial bleeding?', 'Slow and even flow|Steady, slow, darker-red bleeding|Spurting or pulsating, often bright-red blood|Bleeding that is always concealed', 2, 'Arterial bleeding may spurt or pulsate and is usually bright red.'],
  ['Which description is associated with venous bleeding?', 'Bright-red pulsating blood|No bleeding from a damaged vessel|Slow, even capillary flow|Steady, slower bleeding that is often darker red', 3, 'Venous bleeding is commonly steady and slower, with darker red blood.'],
  ['How is capillary bleeding typically described?', 'Slow and even flow|Pulsating bright-red spurting|A concealed haematoma only|A large-volume internal haemorrhage', 0, 'Capillary bleeding is usually slow and even.'],
  ['What does the “blood on the floor and four more” approach include?', 'Only the visible wound and the patient history|The chest, abdomen, pelvis and long bones|Only the head, neck, chest and arms|Only the four limbs', 1, 'The deck uses the visible blood plus assessment of the chest, abdomen, pelvis and long bones.'],
  ['Which finding may suggest internal bleeding?', 'Warm skin with a normal heart rate|Improved level of consciousness|Pallor, cool skin, tachycardia or shallow rapid breathing|A small superficial graze only', 2, 'Pallor, cool skin, tachycardia and shallow rapid breathing may indicate concealed blood loss.'],
  ['Which term describes vomiting blood?', 'Haematuria|Melaena|Haematochezia|Haematemesis', 3, 'Haematemesis is blood appearing in vomit.'],
  ['What should be included in general bleeding management?', 'Safety, PPE, control of catastrophic haemorrhage and an ABCDE assessment|Only a wound photograph|Only a pain score|Waiting for hospital before controlling bleeding', 0, 'The deck prioritises safety and PPE, catastrophic haemorrhage control and the ABCDEs.'],
  ['Where should an arterial tourniquet be placed?', 'Around the neck above the clavicle|Directly on the skin, as low as possible but at least 5 cm or 2 inches above the bleeding site|Over the wound itself with no space|Below the bleeding site', 1, 'The deck advises placing the tourniquet directly on skin, low as possible but above the bleeding site, with about 5 cm or 2 inches of clearance.'],
  ['What may be done if bleeding continues after applying a tourniquet?', 'Loosen it repeatedly to check the wound|Remove it and apply only a plaster|Apply a second tourniquet if required|Place the tourniquet below the wound', 2, 'A second tourniquet may be applied if bleeding continues.'],
  ['What is the scope position for haemostatic gauze in the supplied deck?', 'It is routine AAP equipment|It should be used for every minor wound|It replaces direct pressure in all patients|It is outside AAP scope; authorised T/EMTs may use it only under direct supervision of a qualified colleague', 3, 'The deck states that haemostatic gauze is not AAP scope of practice and describes supervised use by T/EMTs.'],
  ['How should the haemorrhage-control ladder be used?', 'Start at the level appropriate to the bleeding and do not use a simple dressing for an exsanguinating patient|Always start with a plaster|Always start with elevation only|Delay control until the wound has been fully examined', 0, 'JRCALC presents haemorrhage control as a ladder and the deck warns against starting with a simple dressing when the patient is exsanguinating.'],
  ['How should an amputated limb or body part be stored?', 'Directly on dry ice|In a moist dressing, inside a bag and on ice if possible without direct ice contact|Loose on the floor beside the patient|In hot water', 1, 'The deck advises a moist dressing, a bag and ice if possible, while avoiding direct contact between tissue and ice.'],
  ['Approximately how much is 30 ml of blood?', 'One litre|One standard can|About six teaspoons|About ten intravenous bottles', 2, 'The deck equates approximately 30 ml with six teaspoons.'],
  ['Which fracture is associated with the largest approximate blood-loss range in the deck?', 'A rib fracture|A radius or ulna fracture|A humerus fracture|A pelvic fracture', 3, 'The deck gives the pelvis an estimated blood-loss range of 1000–5000 ml.'],
  ['What describes a category 1 pressure ulcer?', 'Non-blanchable erythema of intact skin|Partial-thickness skin loss with an exposed tendon|Full-thickness loss with exposed bone|A wound with no skin change', 0, 'Category 1 is non-blanchable erythema of intact skin.'],
  ['Which statement about pressure ulcers is correct?', 'They occur only on the hands|Category 4 involves exposed bone, tendon or muscle, while an unstageable ulcer has an obscured base|The patient should be positioned directly on the ulcer to relieve pressure elsewhere|They never raise safeguarding concerns', 1, 'Category 4 exposes deeper structures. An unstageable ulcer has a base obscured by slough or eschar, and high-grade ulcers may raise safeguarding concerns.']
]);
addQuestions('extrication-light-rescue', [
  ['What is the main aim of extrication?', 'To move every patient before assessing the scene|To avoid involving other emergency services|To free and remove a patient safely from entrapment or a restricted location|To replace all clinical assessment', 2, 'Extrication is the planned process of freeing and removing a patient while managing clinical and scene risks.'],
  ['How should light rescue be approached?', 'As a last resort within agreed ways of working|As the first response to every difficult location|Without considering specialist support|As an activity anyone can improvise', 0, 'The deck describes light rescue as a last resort and stresses agreed ways of working and safety.'],
  ['What should never be risked during a rescue?', 'The time taken to complete paperwork|The appearance of the equipment|The patient preferred route home|The safety of the rescuer or the patient', 3, 'The deck repeatedly states never to risk your own safety or the patient safety.'],
  ['Who decides the appropriate management and handling before, during and after a rescue attempt?', 'The nearest bystander|The senior clinician|The vehicle driver|The first person to arrive', 1, 'The senior clinician is responsible for deciding the appropriate management and handling.'],
  ['When should rescue be considered?', 'Only when the patient is stable|When the patient must be removed from harm safely or the patient condition requires it|Only when no specialist agency is available|Only after all treatment is complete', 1, 'The deck identifies rescue as necessary when the patient can be removed from harm safely or the condition requires it.'],
  ['What should staff do if HART or the Fire Service is available for a rescue?', 'Attempt the rescue first to save time|Ignore the specialist agency|Enter the hazard without PPE|Use the trained specialist support and do not attempt a have-a-go rescue', 3, 'The deck says not to attempt rescue if HART or Fire Service is available and never to have a go to avoid calling them.'],
  ['What does SCENE help provide?', 'A systematic scene assessment|A definitive hospital diagnosis|A replacement for SBAR|A method of calculating drug doses', 0, 'SCENE promotes a systematic assessment and reduces the chance of overlooking red flags.'],
  ['What does the S in SCENE represent?', 'Severity|Surgery|Safety|Splinting', 2, 'SCENE begins with Safety.'],
  ['What does the C in SCENE represent?', 'Circulation only|Clinical discharge|Cardiac history only|Cause or mechanism', 3, 'The C represents Cause or mechanism.'],
  ['What does the final E in SCENE represent?', 'External bleeding only|Extra resources|End of treatment|Equipment disposal only', 1, 'The final E identifies Extra resources needed.'],
  ['What is the purpose of a dynamic risk assessment?', 'To remove the need for reassessment|To decide that all rescues are safe|To identify who may be harmed, what further action or resources are needed, who should act and how urgent it is|To replace communication with other agencies', 2, 'The dynamic risk assessment asks who may be harmed, what is being done, what more is needed, who should act and how urgent it is.'],
  ['What should happen to a dynamic risk assessment during a rescue?', 'It should be constantly reassessed and re-evaluated|It should be completed once and never changed|It should stop when the patient is reached|It should be completed only by a bystander', 0, 'The deck emphasises constant reassessment and re-evaluation.'],
  ['Which factor may affect the speed of extrication?', 'Only the patient age|Number of patients, crew, resources, equipment and time-critical features|Only the weather forecast|Only the hospital parking arrangements', 1, 'The deck lists patients, crew, extra resources, equipment and time-critical features.'],
  ['What is the purpose of SBAR during a rescue request?', 'To replace a primary survey|To document only the patient name|To provide a long-term rehabilitation plan|To communicate the situation, background, assessment and recommendation clearly', 3, 'SBAR structures the request for help and communicates urgency and required support.'],
  ['Which information belongs in the Situation part of SBAR?', 'The full past medical history only|The final hospital diagnosis|Why the team was called and a brief overview of findings and hazards|The equipment inventory only', 2, 'The Situation explains why the team was called and summarises findings and identified hazards.'],
  ['Which information belongs in the Recommendation part of SBAR?', 'The assistance required and its urgency|Only the patient date of birth|Only the mechanism of injury|Only the number of observations taken', 0, 'The Recommendation states the assistance required and how urgent it is, including partner agencies where needed.'],
  ['Which resources may HART provide during a rescue incident?', 'Only routine transport home|Only fire extinguishers|Only administrative support|Specialist personnel, PPE, vehicles, command and control and clinical interventions', 3, 'The deck identifies HART specialist support, advanced PPE and vehicles, command and control, triage and clinical interventions.'],
  ['According to the deck, which role does Fire and Rescue provide at the scene?', 'Only recording the patient name|Scene safety and fire and rescue support|Only providing analgesia|Only arranging hospital appointments', 1, 'The deck identifies Fire and Rescue Services as responsible for scene safety and providing fire and rescue support.'],
  ['What may the MPS provide during a rescue incident?', 'Public order, scene security, entry to premises and safety support|Only clinical airway management|Only specialist spinal immobilisation|Only transport to hospital', 0, 'The deck lists public order, scene security, entry to premises, safety support and crime-scene continuity.'],
  ['What is USAR trained to deal with?', 'Only routine road traffic collisions|Only minor household injuries|Collapsed or unsafe structures, work at height and confined spaces|Only waterborne illness', 2, 'USAR is trained for collapsed structures, unsafe structures, work at height and confined spaces.'],
  ['Which item is listed as light-rescue equipment?', 'A routine medication chart|A food thermometer|A hearing aid|An orthopaedic stretcher or rescue board', 3, 'The equipment list includes orthopaedic stretchers and rescue boards.'],
  ['Which item is identified as HART equipment in the deck?', 'The Kendrick Extrication Device|A standard blanket|A routine pulse oximeter|A hospital meal tray', 0, 'The deck identifies the Kendrick Extrication Device as HART equipment.'],
  ['What should equipment selection be based on?', 'Only the colour of the equipment|The patient, entrapment, environment, access, egress and available team|Only the patient age|Only the nearest hospital', 1, 'Equipment and planning must reflect the patient, hazard, route, team and rescue requirements.'],
  ['When may self-extrication be appropriate for a patient?', 'Whenever the patient asks, regardless of injury|Only when no assessment has occurred|When the patient can understand instructions, move safely, use a safe route and has no overriding mechanism or injury concern|Only after specialist rescue is refused', 2, 'Self-extrication depends on patient ability, understanding, mechanism, injuries, hazards and a safe route.'],
  ['What should remain continuous during patient movement?', 'The patient social history only|The equipment inventory|The police investigation only|Clinical assessment and management', 3, 'The patient condition remains the team responsibility throughout the rescue, with continuous assessment and management.'],
  ['Which factors should be considered when planning immobilisation and patient movement?', 'Only whether the patient is comfortable|Suspected injury, body support, movement, equipment and the need to remove the patient from harm|Only the distance to the ambulance|Only the weather', 1, 'Immobilisation planning must balance suspected injury, movement precautions, equipment and safe removal from harm.'],
  ['When may crash-helmet removal be considered?', 'When required for airway or head assessment under trained local guidance|To make the patient more comfortable in every case|To avoid calling a specialist agency|To complete paperwork more quickly', 0, 'The deck identifies helmet removal as a learning area; any removal should follow trained local guidance and senior clinician direction.'],
  ['Which potential complication should be considered during crash-helmet removal?', 'Guaranteed improvement in spinal stability|Prevention of all airway problems|Unwanted head and neck movement or worsening airway compromise|Improved circulation in every patient', 2, 'Potential risks include unwanted movement, aggravating spinal injury and worsening airway management.'],
  ['Which combination of findings should trigger early escalation and prioritised rescue planning?', 'A stable patient with no hazards|Airway compromise, shock, reduced consciousness or a dangerous environment|A completed equipment check only|A normal observation with no mechanism', 1, 'Time-critical features include airway or breathing compromise, haemorrhage, shock, reduced consciousness and dangerous environments.'],
  ['What should happen after the patient has been extricated?', 'Stop monitoring because the rescue is complete|Remove all documentation|Assume no injury was caused by the entrapment|Repeat assessment, reassess injuries and hand over the mechanism, findings and interventions', 3, 'After rescue, reassess the patient, check for hidden injury, review observations and provide a structured handover.']
]);
